/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     PLENIX SETTINGS DATA — window globals (plain JS, loaded only by the Settings page, after plenix-app-data.js)

     The data only Settings reads (issue 882): the feature areas, the capability layers and documentation, the data
     schema, the terminology tables and the product-view texts. The other suite pages load plenix-app-data.js alone.
       window.FEATURES   window.CAP_LAYERS   window.CAP_DOCS   window.SCHEMA
       window.TERM_LAYERS   window.TERM_HIERARCHY   window.TERM_EXAMPLE
       window.PV_PROBLEMS   window.PV_PHILOSOPHY   window.PV_UNIFIES   window.PV_MESSAGING   window.PV_USECASES
       window.PV_NOT   window.PV_NAME_ROWS   window.PV_FIT   window.PV_MODEL
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  window.FEATURES=[
    {area:"AI Agents",color:"red",items:[
      {label:"AI Agent creation and management",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A user wants to deploy an AI persona that responds to messages across connected channels autonomously. An AI Agent is a configured LLM instance (e.g. Claude Sonnet) with a name, avatar, system prompt, tone settings, and permitted tools (web search, calendar lookup, file retrieval, contact lookup, task creation). The Agents screen lists all configured agents as cards showing: name, short description, assigned channels, and Active/Paused status. Clicking an agent opens its full configuration."},
      {label:"Agent system prompt and persona",status:"planned",desc:"The agent configuration includes a multi-line system prompt editor where the user writes the agent's instructions, personality, role, and constraints. Preset persona templates are offered as starting points: Customer Support, Personal Assistant, Research Helper, Sales Assistant. A 'Test agent' button opens a live chat preview panel so the user can interact with the agent before deploying it to any channel."},
      {label:"Agent tool permissions",status:"planned",desc:"A toggle list controls what the agent is allowed to do: Web Search, Calendar Read, Task Creation, Contact Lookup, File Search, Email Draft, and custom Webhooks. Each tool shows a brief description of what it enables. Disabling a tool removes it from the agent's available functions at the API level, not just at the prompt level — so it is a hard constraint, not just an instruction."},
      {label:"Agent–channel assignment",status:"planned",desc:"A user may have multiple agents (e.g. customer-facing support and internal productivity) and multiple channels (Telegram, WhatsApp, Discord). The assignment UI lets the user assign agents to channels via dropdown on each channel card. One channel can only have one active agent at a time. One agent can be assigned to multiple channels simultaneously with consistent behavior across all."},
      {label:"Agent conversation logs",status:"planned",desc:"Each agent has a Logs tab: chronological conversations showing who sent the message, which channel it came from, the user's message, and the agent's reply. Filterable by channel, date range, and outcome (success / escalated / error). Clicking a row opens the full thread. A 'Flag for review' action marks a conversation for follow-up."},
      {label:"Human escalation and handoff",status:"planned",desc:"When the agent cannot confidently answer (based on a confidence threshold or keyword triggers), it escalates to a human. The human receives a notification in Plenix Messaging with the full conversation context pre-loaded. They can reply directly and return control to the agent when done. The agent monitors the handoff and resumes autonomously when the human marks it resolved."}
    ]},
    {area:"AI Chat",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Conversational AI interface — the primary AI entry point",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to interact with an AI assistant in natural language to get things done — drafting content, analyzing data, writing code, researching topics, processing files — without leaving Plenix. The AI Chat section is a full conversational interface: a message thread on the left and an artifact/output panel on the right. The user types or speaks a request, the AI streams its response in real time, and any generated files (HTML pages, documents, charts, code) open in an interactive preview panel on the right. This is not a simple chatbot — it is the primary AI assistant surface for Plenix, with access to all connected data (files, contacts, calendar, tasks, notes) via the Connectors layer. The AI works as an assistant to the user, not autonomously."},
      {label:"Chat input bar — text, file upload, voice, @ mentions",status:"planned",desc:"The input bar sits at the bottom of the chat. It contains: a text field for natural language requests; a Send button (or Enter key); a Stop button that appears during streaming to cancel the current response; a paperclip/file upload button to attach images (PNG, JPG, GIF, WebP), documents (PDF, TXT, CSV), and code files (HTML, CSS, JS, Python, etc.) for the AI to read and process; a microphone button for voice input; and an @ mention trigger that opens a menu of available skills and connectors by name (e.g. '@web-search Find the latest React best practices'). The input bar is always visible and accessible from anywhere in the chat thread."},
      {label:"Voice input — dictate messages with live waveform",status:"planned",desc:"Tapping the microphone icon transforms the input bar into a voice recording interface. A live waveform visualization shows audio levels in real time. A timer shows the recording duration (up to 2 minutes). Two buttons appear: a checkmark to stop recording and transcribe, and an X to cancel and discard. After recording, a 'Transcribing...' indicator appears while the audio is processed. The transcribed text populates the input field where the user can review and edit before sending, or tap the mic again to append more dictation. Uses a high-accuracy speech-to-text model supporting multiple languages. Works in all major browsers after the first microphone permission grant."},
      {label:"Streaming responses — thinking indicator, tool execution, progressive output",status:"planned",desc:"Responses stream token by token in real time. During streaming the user sees: an animated thinking indicator while the AI plans its approach; status messages like 'Running search...' or 'Generating file...'; tool execution — each tool call and its result streams inline so the user can see exactly what the AI is doing step by step; text content appearing progressively; and the artifact panel opening on the right as soon as a file is generated. The user never waits for a complete response before seeing progress. A red Stop button appears during streaming and cancels the response immediately when clicked."},
      {label:"Queued follow-up messages",status:"planned",desc:"The user can send additional messages while the AI is still processing a previous request. Instead of waiting, they type the follow-up and send — it enters a queue. Two modes: Automatic (the AI picks up the next queued message and processes it without user interaction; the queue continues even if the browser is closed) and Manual (the next queued message is promoted into the input field for review and editing before sending). This enables multi-step workflows where the user queues several instructions upfront and lets the AI work through them in sequence."},
      {label:"Artifacts — interactive previews for generated files",status:"planned",desc:"When the AI generates an HTML page, SVG, PDF, chart, or other file, an artifact panel opens on the right side of the chat with an interactive preview. An artifact chip appears in the chat message so the panel can be reopened later. Generated HTML pages are fully interactive — buttons, forms, and JavaScript all execute in the preview panel. The user can switch between Preview and Code views, download the file, or copy the source. This panel is also where dynamic dashboards render when the AI produces data analyses or visual summaries."},
      {label:"Dynamic dashboards — inline data visualizations",status:"planned",desc:"When the user asks for data analysis, metric comparisons, or visual summaries (e.g. 'analyze my project costs for Q1' or 'build a dashboard comparing this month's tasks vs last month'), the AI renders an interactive dashboard directly in the chat. Dashboards are composed of reusable widgets that assemble into a layout and stream progressively — the user sees the dashboard build piece by piece. Widget types: KPI cards (headline metrics with growth badges), area/bar/line charts, tables (comparisons, breakdowns), alert callouts for key findings, and progress bars for distribution data. All widgets can reference data from connected sources (Google Sheets, files, task data, calendar events)."},
      {label:"Text-to-speech — summarize and read aloud",status:"planned",desc:"A three-dot menu on any AI message includes a 'Summarize & read aloud' option. The AI automatically condenses long or complex responses into concise spoken sentences and plays them back as natural-sounding audio. Useful for consuming long outputs without reading. Clicking again stops playback. The summary is generated specifically for audio consumption — shorter and more conversational than the written response."},
      {label:"Thread management — history, pinning, sharing",status:"planned",desc:"Each conversation is a persistent thread with an auto-generated title (derived from the first message) and timestamp. The left sidebar lists recent threads grouped by recency. Actions per thread: continue (resume any previous conversation with full context), start fresh (New Chat creates a new thread), pin (keeps frequently used threads at the top of the sidebar). Thread sharing: a share icon generates a read-only public link. Anyone with the link can view the full conversation including messages, artifacts, and generated files without needing a Plenix account. Sharing can be revoked at any time, immediately invalidating the URL."},
      {label:"Extended thinking — max reasoning mode",status:"planned",desc:"For complex tasks — multi-step code generation, intricate analysis, long-horizon planning — an 'Extended thinking' mode gives the AI more time to reason through the problem before responding. Enabled via a toggle in the model selector. During extended thinking, the AI works through intermediate reasoning steps internally before streaming its final answer. More credits are consumed per response. If the thinking budget is exhausted mid-response, the AI automatically retries to ensure a complete answer. Visible to the user as a longer thinking indicator before the first token streams."}
    ]},
    {area:"Automations",color:"purple",items:[
      {label:"Scheduled tasks — overview and grouping",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to offload repetitive daily work — morning briefings, inbox triage, end-of-day summaries, competitor monitoring — to run automatically on a schedule. The Automations screen lists all configured tasks grouped into four categories: Daily (fixed-time daily tasks), Monitoring (continuous or periodic background checks), Reports (periodic summaries compiled from connected data sources), and Automation (event-triggered workflows). A left sidebar nav jumps between categories. A '+ New task' button opens the task creation flow. Each task is a card with an emoji icon, name, and one-line description. These are user-defined automations with AI as assistant — not autonomous agents acting independently. The user initiates, configures, and reviews all outputs."},
      {label:"Daily tasks — Morning Briefing, Standup Prep, End-of-Day, Inbox Triage",status:"planned",desc:"Morning Briefing compiles at a set time: today's weather, filtered headlines, calendar events, top 3 priority tasks — delivered as a digest to Notes or notifications. Standup Prep scans recent calendar events, task completions, and file activity to draft a bullet-point standup the user can review and send. End-of-Day Wrap-up summarizes progress against today's tasks, flags open items, and suggests tomorrow's priorities. Inbox Triage reviews unread email and surfaces highest-urgency messages first, suggesting which to reply to, defer, or archive."},
      {label:"Monitoring tasks — Competitor Watch, Trending Topics, Tech Radar, Dependency Check",status:"planned",desc:"Competitor Watch runs daily or weekly and searches the web for news, product updates, pricing changes, and press releases from named competitors — delivering a digest to Notes. Trending Topics monitors communities, news sources, and social feeds for emerging industry discussions. Tech Radar watches for new releases and security advisories for the specific packages and frameworks in the user's tech stack (informed by connected GitHub repos). Dependency Check audits project dependencies against vulnerability databases and flags outdated packages, optionally creating a task to resolve each."},
      {label:"Report tasks — Weekly Review, Code Quality, Git Activity, User Feedback Digest",status:"planned",desc:"Weekly Review runs every Friday: summarizes the week's commits, completed tasks, open items, and suggested next-week priorities as a structured note. Code Quality Report runs lint and type-check tools on connected repos and summarizes errors and trends. Git Activity Summary compiles recent commits, active branches, and hotspot files. User Feedback Digest aggregates recurring themes from connected feedback sources (support tickets, app reviews, surveys) and surfaces sentiment trends and top pain points."},
      {label:"Automation tasks — event-triggered workflows",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants Plenix to react to events automatically — new email from a client creates a task, GitHub PR opened notifies a contact, calendar event ends prompts for notes. Each automation has a trigger (event from a connected service), a condition (optional filter, e.g. 'only if sender is in Clients group'), and one or more actions (create task, send message, create note, update calendar). A visual trigger → condition → action builder makes these configurable without code. The user reviews automation outputs before finalization."},
      {label:"Task creation and configuration",status:"planned",desc:"Clicking '+ New task' opens a creation panel: name, category selector (Daily/Monitoring/Reports/Automation), schedule picker (specific time, recurring interval, or event trigger), description, data sources to read from (informed by active Connectors), and output destination (Notes folder, Notification, Email draft, Task). Preset templates (Morning Briefing, Competitor Watch, etc.) offered as starting points. The user can preview a dry run before enabling. All tasks can be paused, edited, or deleted at any time."}
    ]},
    {area:"Bookmarks & Browser Capture",color:"purple",items:[
      {label:"Bookmarks section — save and organize web content",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to save web pages, articles, videos, tools, and reference links for later — organized into collections rather than a flat browser bookmark list. Plenix's Bookmarks section stores saved URLs as rich entries with: the page title, URL, a thumbnail or favicon, the date saved, tags, a personal note, and the collection it belongs to. Collections replace the browser's folders concept: the user creates named collections (Reading List, Watch Later, Research, Suppliers, Inspiration, Tools) and assigns bookmarks to one or more. The sidebar shows all collections with item counts. A search bar filters by title, URL, tags, or note content. Each bookmark can be opened, annotated further, moved to a different collection, or converted to a Task (useful when a saved page requires action — e.g. 'Fill out this application')."},
      {label:"Browser bookmarklet — one-click save from any web page",status:"planned",desc:"The primary capture method is a browser bookmarklet — a small JavaScript snippet saved as a browser bookmark. When the user clicks it on any web page, it extracts the page title and URL and sends them to Plenix, creating a new bookmark entry in the configured target collection. Setup: the user generates a personalized bookmarklet in Settings → Bookmarks by selecting a target collection (or Inbox) and copying their access token. The generated bookmarklet code is dragged to the browser bookmarks bar. From that point, clicking it on any page saves the page instantly — no form to fill, no modal to dismiss. The bookmarklet works in Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge. It is the fastest possible save action: one click, zero friction, no context switch."},
      {label:"Bookmarklet technical details — metadata extraction and API call",status:"planned",desc:"When triggered, the bookmarklet executes in the context of the current web page. It reads document.title and window.location.href, then constructs a POST request to the Plenix task/bookmark creation API endpoint with the captured metadata and the user's access token as an authorization header. The server creates the bookmark entry and returns a success response. The bookmarklet shows a brief visual confirmation (a small toast or color flash on the bookmark bar icon) to indicate success. No additional page content is extracted — body text, images, and cookies are not read. The entire script is a few lines of JavaScript encoded as a data: URI in the bookmark's URL field."},
      {label:"CSP limitations and browser extension alternative",status:"planned",desc:"Bookmarklets operate within the security context of the host web page. Some websites implement a Content Security Policy (CSP) that blocks outgoing network requests from inline scripts — these sites will cause the bookmarklet to fail with a 'Failed to fetch' or 'Failed to load' error. This is a browser security restriction, not a Plenix bug, and affects all bookmarklet-based tools. Affected sites include some major platforms that use strict CSP headers. The solution is a Plenix browser extension (planned for Chrome and Firefox) which runs in a privileged extension context rather than the page context and is not subject to page-level CSP restrictions. The extension provides the same one-click save behavior with more reliable cross-site compatibility, plus additional capabilities like selecting text to save as a quote alongside the URL."},
      {label:"Plenix browser extension — enhanced capture with CSP bypass",status:"planned",desc:"The Plenix browser extension (Chrome Web Store and Firefox Add-ons) replaces the bookmarklet for users who need more reliable capture. The extension runs as a browser extension in a privileged context, bypassing page-level CSP restrictions that block bookmarklets. Additional capabilities beyond the bookmarklet: save selected text as a quote alongside the URL (useful for saving a specific passage from an article); detect the main article content on editorial sites and save a clean reader-mode version; capture media links (YouTube videos, podcast episodes) with the media title and embed URL; add a note at the time of saving without opening Plenix; choose the target collection from a popup without pre-configuring the bookmarklet; and show a preview of the captured item before confirming. The extension requires the same access token as the bookmarklet and connects to the same Plenix API."},
      {label:"Reading list and Watch Later — curated queues",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user saves articles and videos faster than they consume them. Without structure, bookmarks pile up into an undifferentiated mass. Plenix provides two first-class queue collections pre-created for every account: Reading List (for articles, blog posts, documentation, long-form content) and Watch Later (for YouTube videos, conference talks, tutorials, short films). Items in these collections are displayed as a prioritized queue — not an alphabetical list. The user can reorder items by dragging, mark items as read/watched (which moves them to an archive rather than deleting), and set a reading/viewing target (e.g. 'read 2 articles per day'). Progress is tracked: the Reading List shows how many items were consumed this week vs added. Items older than a configurable threshold (e.g. 90 days) are flagged as stale so the list stays actionable."},
      {label:"Inbox area — unsorted capture before organizing",status:"planned",desc:"The Bookmark Inbox is a holding area for items captured before the user has decided which collection they belong to. It mirrors the Tasks Inbox pattern: capture fast, organize later. When the bookmarklet or extension is used without a pre-configured target collection, items land in the Inbox. The Inbox view shows all unsorted items with a quick triage interface: the user can assign a collection, add tags, write a note, convert to a task, or delete — for each item in sequence, similar to email triage. A count badge on the Bookmarks nav icon shows how many unsorted Inbox items are waiting. The Inbox is intentionally kept separate from curated collections so that the Reading List stays clean and intentional rather than becoming a dumping ground."},
      {label:"Convert bookmark to task",status:"planned",desc:"Any bookmark can be promoted to a Task with one click. Use cases: a saved job posting becomes a task ('Apply to this role'); a saved form becomes a task ('Complete supplier registration'); a saved article becomes a task ('Read and summarize for team'). The task is created with the bookmark's title as the task title, the URL in the task notes, and the user can immediately set a due date and priority. The original bookmark remains in the Bookmarks section and a link to the associated task appears on the bookmark card. This bidirectional link means the task's notes always contain the source URL and the bookmark always shows whether action has been taken."},
      {label:"Bookmarks search and full-text indexing",status:"planned",desc:"The search bar in the Bookmarks section searches across title, URL, personal notes, and tags. For bookmarks where a clean article body was captured (via the browser extension's reader mode), full-text search also searches the article content — finding bookmarks by a phrase mentioned in the article even if it is not in the title or notes. Search results are ranked by recency and relevance. A 'find similar' action on any bookmark uses AI to surface other saved items with related content — useful for discovering connections between research items saved weeks apart. Bookmarks can also be found via the global search bar in the Plenix header, which returns bookmark results alongside tasks, notes, and contacts."}
    ]},
    {area:"Calendar",color:"brown",items:[
      {label:"Month view",status:"done",desc:"A standard 7-column grid showing all days in the current month. Each cell has a minimum height of 78px and can grow to fit multiple events. Today has a distinct background. The selected day gets a blue border and selection background. Events are rendered as colored pills with a left border in the event's calendar color. Cells showing more than 2 events show a '+N more' label."},
      {label:"Week view",status:"done",desc:"A 7-column time grid running from 8 AM to 6 PM in 60px-high hour rows. Events are plotted into the correct hour slot. Today's column gets a subtle blue tint. Clicking any day number in the column header navigates directly to the Day view for that date."},
      {label:"Day view",status:"done",desc:"A single-day timeline with a large date number and day name at the top. Each hour from 8 AM to 6 PM is a 70px row. Events render as full-width blocks with title, time, and calendar name. All-day events are listed separately. The Today button jumps to this view for today."},
      {label:"Event creation — start/end time, all-day, recurrence, color, project link",status:"planned",desc:"Events are created from any calendar view by clicking a time slot (Day/Week view) or a date cell (Month view). The event creation panel collects: Title, Start time and End time (or All-day toggle — all-day events display at the top of the day rather than in the time grid), Recurrence pattern (Daily / Weekly / Monthly / Yearly — with optional end date or occurrence count), Color (individual color override, or inherits the project color if linked to a project), Project association (links the event to a project so it appears in that project's timeline and is color-matched), Description/notes, and Location or video conference link (auto-detected if a meet/zoom/teams URL is pasted). Created events are stored in Plenix's local event store and optionally pushed to a connected calendar provider if two-way sync is enabled in future."},
      {label:"Recurring events — defined recurrence patterns",status:"planned",desc:"Events can repeat on a defined schedule: Daily (every day, or every N days), Weekly (on selected days of the week — e.g. every Monday and Wednesday), Monthly (on a specific date — e.g. the 15th of every month, or on a weekday — e.g. the first Monday of every month), and Yearly (e.g. an annual review every January 1st). Each recurrence rule follows the iCalendar RRULE standard, ensuring compatibility with exported ICS files and external calendar apps. Editing a recurring event offers three options: edit this occurrence only, edit this and all future occurrences, or edit all occurrences. Deleting follows the same three-option pattern."},
      {label:"All-day events — separate display above time grid",status:"planned",desc:"Events marked as all-day are displayed in a dedicated all-day row at the top of the Day and Week views, above the timed hour slots. This ensures they are always visible without taking up space in the timeline grid. Multiple all-day events on the same day are stacked vertically in the all-day row. In Month view, all-day events appear as full-width pills spanning the relevant days. Tasks flagged as due on a specific date (without a time) are also shown in the all-day row as task markers, distinguishable from events by a different visual treatment."},
      {label:"Event color customization and project color inheritance",status:"planned",desc:"Each event can be assigned an individual color from a palette. If an event is linked to a project, it can inherit the project's color automatically — making all project-related events immediately identifiable on the calendar without manual coloring. The color inheritance rule: if an event has an individually set color, that takes priority; if no color is set and the event is project-linked, the project color is used; if neither, the event uses the source calendar's color. Color is shown on the event pill in Month view, on the event block in Week/Day view, and on the event dot in the sidebar upcoming list."},
      {label:"Task integration — due dates on the calendar timeline",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks with assigned due dates appear directly on the Calendar timeline alongside scheduled events, providing a unified planning view. Date-only tasks appear as markers in the all-day row. Tasks with a specific time appear as schedulable blocks in the time grid. A visual distinction separates tasks from events: tasks have a checkbox overlay on the calendar block, and checking it completes the task without opening the task detail. This integration means the Calendar is a complete picture of the day — not just meetings but all commitments. Tasks completed from the calendar are reflected immediately in the Tasks section."},
      {label:"Today and Tomorrow views — hour-by-hour daily planning",status:"planned",desc:"The calendar defaults to a Today view — a vertical hour-by-hour timeline of the current day showing calendar events and any time blocks the user has placed. On desktop when the panel is expanded, a Tomorrow view appears alongside Today, enabling the user to plan the next day while reviewing the current one. This side-by-side layout is designed for the end-of-day planning routine: reviewing what was accomplished today and intentionally scheduling tomorrow before closing out. Mobile shows Today only. Both views show the same event and block data — the difference is purely temporal."},
      {label:"Calendar integrations — supported providers",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user's events already live in Google Calendar, Outlook, or iCloud. Plenix displays these events alongside tasks and time blocks without requiring the user to re-enter anything. Supported providers and protocols: Google Calendar (OAuth, real-time sync on desktop), Outlook/Microsoft 365 (OAuth, real-time sync on desktop), iCloud (CalDAV), Proton Calendar (CalDAV or via system calendar workaround — see note), FastMail (CalDAV), Hey (CalDAV), Facebook Events (iCal feed), and any iCal/ICS feed URL (manual subscription). All providers also support CalDAV as a universal fallback. Sync is one-way only (external calendar → Plenix) — Plenix reads events but does not write back to the provider. Two-way sync is planned but not in the current scope."},
      {label:"Sync behavior — frequency and platform differences",status:"planned",desc:"Sync frequency varies by provider and platform. On desktop: Google and Outlook sync in real time via their respective push/subscription APIs, so new events appear in Plenix within seconds of being created. All other providers (iCloud, CalDAV sources, ICS feeds) sync every 5 minutes on a background poll. On mobile: all providers sync automatically every 2 minutes via the native calendar API (iOS EventKit / Android Calendar Provider), which reads from the device's system calendar. A manual sync is always available (pull-to-refresh gesture or a refresh button in the calendar toolbar). The mobile approach mirrors events from the device's system calendar rather than connecting to each provider's API individually — this means any calendar the user has synced to their device (including Proton, FastMail, etc.) is automatically visible in Plenix on mobile without additional configuration."},
      {label:"Mobile calendar — native API integration",status:"planned",desc:"On mobile devices, Plenix reads calendar data through the native system calendar API rather than connecting to each cloud provider directly. On iOS this uses EventKit, which reads from the iOS Calendar app. On Android this uses the Android Calendar Provider, which reads from the device's calendar database. The practical effect is that any calendar the user has already configured on their phone — Google, Outlook, iCloud, Exchange, or any CalDAV source — appears in Plenix automatically once calendar permission is granted. No per-provider OAuth is required on mobile. The user grants Plenix calendar read access once and all synced calendars become available immediately."},
      {label:"Proton Calendar on Android — workaround",status:"planned",desc:"Proton Calendar on Android does not expose events through the Android system calendar interface by default, which means Plenix's native API approach does not read Proton events on Android. The recommended workaround is to use a compatible CalDAV client app (such as DAVx⁵) that connects to Proton Calendar via CalDAV and syncs events into the Android system calendar. Once events are in the system calendar, Plenix reads them normally. This workaround is documented in Plenix's help content for users who need Proton Calendar support on Android. iOS users with Proton Calendar can use the Proton Mail app's calendar export or connect via CalDAV directly in iOS Settings."},
      {label:"Tag filters — event type pills",status:"done",desc:"A row of pill buttons sits between the toolbar and the calendar grid. Each pill represents a unique event type derived from event titles. Clicking a pill filters the entire calendar to only show matching events. The active pill takes on the event's color. Clicking All or re-clicking the active pill clears the filter. Filters combine with calendar toggles in the sidebar."},
      {label:"Calendar sidebar — My Calendars, All Calendars, Import",status:"done",desc:"The left sidebar lists all calendars with a colored square indicator per calendar. 'All Calendars' at the top selects all. Clicking a calendar highlights it and filters the grid. Import Calendar at the bottom (separated by a divider) will open a file picker for .ics import. Upcoming events are listed below."},
      {label:"Video conference detection — one-click join",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: When a meeting has a Zoom, Google Meet, or Teams link in its description or location field, the user should be able to join directly from the calendar event without hunting for the link. Plenix automatically scans event descriptions and location fields for video conference URLs and displays a Join button on the event when one is detected. Supported platforms: Zoom (zoom.us links), Google Meet (meet.google.com links), Microsoft Teams (teams.microsoft.com links), and BBB (BigBlueButton — specifically noted for the Technical University of Munich instance at bbb.rbg.tum.de). The Join button opens the meeting link in a new tab or in the platform's native app if installed. No manual configuration is required — detection is automatic on every event that has a recognized link pattern."},
      {label:"Event-linked notes",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user has a meeting on their calendar and wants to create a note for it — meeting agenda, prep notes, or post-meeting action items — linked directly to the event so it is always findable from the calendar. From any event in Day or Week view, a 'Create note' action opens a note creation panel. A default note name is generated from the event title and date (e.g. 'Contractor call — March 5, 2026'). The user can optionally select a notebook/folder and apply a note template (e.g. 'Meeting notes' template with sections for Attendees, Agenda, Decisions, Action items). Once created, the note is linked to the event — a note icon appears on the event in the calendar view, and clicking it opens the note directly. The event title and date are also stored in the note's metadata for reverse lookup. Currently desktop only; mobile support planned."},
      {label:"Keyboard shortcut — toggle calendar panel",status:"planned",desc:"The calendar panel (or day planning sidebar on desktop) can be toggled open and closed via a keyboard shortcut: Cmd+S on Mac, Ctrl+S on other platforms. This lets the user quickly check their schedule without navigating away from whatever they are working on. The shortcut is documented in the keyboard shortcuts reference panel in Settings."},
      {label:"Visual day timeline — duration-proportional blocks",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A standard task list shows items as equal-height rows regardless of how long each one takes. A 10-minute call looks the same as a 4-hour deep-work session. The visual timeline makes time honest — each task and event is rendered as a colored block whose height is proportional to its duration. A 30-minute task takes up half the vertical space of a 1-hour task. Back-to-back commitments show immediately as crowded. Empty space shows clearly as available time. The user can see at a glance whether their day is realistic before it starts. Calendar events from connected providers appear automatically as blocks alongside manually placed tasks, creating a unified visual picture of the whole day. This is the defining planning insight that list-based views cannot provide."},
      {label:"Drag-and-drop timeline editing",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks and time blocks on the timeline are draggable. Dragging a block vertically moves it to a different time slot. Dragging the bottom edge resizes it (changes the duration). Dragging a task from the Capture inbox onto the timeline assigns it a time and adds it to the day plan. Blocks snap to 15-minute increments by default (configurable: 5, 10, 15, or 30 minutes). Dragging a block over an existing block highlights the conflict. Completed tasks cannot be dragged — they are locked in place as a record of what was done. On mobile, long-press activates drag mode on a block."},
      {label:"Replan — AI reschedules missed or overrun tasks",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: It is 2 PM and three morning tasks were not completed because a meeting ran long. The user needs to redistribute the unfinished work into the remaining day without manually dragging each block. The Replan function looks at all incomplete tasks from the current day, the remaining available time (free gaps between now and end of day), and each task's estimated duration, then proposes a revised schedule. The user sees the proposed rearrangement as a before/after timeline and can accept, modify, or reject it. Replan also works cross-day — if there is no room today, it can push tasks to tomorrow or the next available day. The AI accounts for task priority (higher-priority tasks get earlier slots) and recurring commitments (does not schedule over a daily recurring task)."},
      {label:"AI day planning — draft a full schedule from natural language",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to plan their day but does not want to manually place every block. AI day planning generates a full schedule from a brief description: 'I have a supplier call at 10, need to work on the project proposal, exercise, and finish the tax documents — I want to be done by 6 PM.' The AI reads connected calendar events (fills in the supplier call automatically), estimates durations for each task (pulling from the task's time-estimate field if set, or using AI estimation), and produces a proposed timeline. The user reviews the proposal on the visual timeline and can drag blocks to adjust. Multiple images can be attached to the AI prompt (e.g. a photo of a handwritten to-do list, a screenshot of a meeting agenda) and the AI incorporates that information into the schedule."},
      {label:"Recurring tasks and routines — daily structure that persists",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user has a morning routine (8:00 Rise, 8:15 Exercise, 9:00 Review priorities) and recurring work rituals (Daily standup at 9:30, Weekly review every Friday at 4 PM, Monthly supplier check-in first Monday of the month). These should appear on the timeline automatically without re-creating them every day. Recurring tasks are created with a repeat schedule: daily, weekly on specific days, every N days, monthly on a date, monthly on a weekday (e.g. first Monday), yearly. Each occurrence is an independent instance — completing today's does not affect tomorrow's. Skipping one occurrence is possible without breaking the series. The routine framing (Rise and Shine / Wind Down anchor tasks) bookends the day so the timeline always has a clear start and end point."},
      {label:"Energy monitor — plan around personal energy levels",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Cognitive demanding tasks (writing, analysis, complex decisions) are best done when the user's energy is highest, while low-effort tasks (admin, email) can fill low-energy periods. The Energy Monitor lets the user define their typical daily energy curve — a simple high/medium/low profile across the hours of the day (e.g. High: 9–12, Medium: 2–4, Low: 12–2 and after 5). This profile is shown as a subtle color gradient on the timeline background. When AI day planning is used, it schedules high-priority, cognitively demanding tasks during high-energy periods automatically. The user can update their energy profile anytime. A daily energy log lets the user rate their actual energy at the end of each day, over time building a personalized baseline that improves AI scheduling accuracy."},
      {label:"Color coding and icons — visual task categorization",status:"planned",desc:"Every task and time block on the timeline has a color and an icon. Color is used for categorization: the user assigns colors to categories (Work = blue, Personal = green, Health = orange, Finance = red) and each task inherits its category's color, making the timeline scannable at a glance. Icons are chosen per task from a library (hundreds of icons covering activities, objects, emotions, work types) — a task titled 'Gym' gets a dumbbell icon, 'Call Marcus' gets a phone icon, 'Write proposal' gets a document icon. Color and icon are independent — two tasks can share a color but have different icons. The visual density of the timeline (how much is packed in) and the color distribution (how much work vs personal) give the user an immediate read of their day's character before it starts."},
      {label:"Pomodoro focus timer — built into timeline tasks",status:"planned",desc:"Any task on the timeline can be started in Pomodoro mode. Tapping 'Start focus' on a task launches a countdown timer (default 25 minutes work / 5 minute break, configurable) visible as a Live Activity on mobile and a menu bar indicator on desktop. When the work interval ends, a gentle sound plays and the break timer starts automatically. After 4 Pomodoros, a longer break (15–30 minutes) is suggested. The completed Pomodoro count is shown on the task and in the day summary. The timer is non-intrusive — it runs in the background and does not block the rest of the app. This integrates with the time-estimate field: if a task is estimated at 2 hours, the system suggests 4 Pomodoros and tracks progress toward the estimate."}
    ]},
    {area:"Capture",color:"brown",items:[
      {label:"AI File Naming",status:"done",desc:"The Capture button in the header opens a modal that lets you select an unorganized file from a list of candidates. The AI reads the file's context (date, project association, content type, description) and generates a structured filename in the format: YYYY-MM-DD — Project — Type — Description.ext. Each part of the filename is color-coded (date in blue, project in orange, type in green, description in purple) so you can immediately understand the naming logic. You can accept the name and save the file to a suggested folder."}
    ]},
    {area:"Channels",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Channel card dashboard — grid layout",status:"planned",desc:"The Channels screen renders a 3-column card grid. Each card shows: platform branded icon (~48px), platform name, bot identifier, Connected/Disconnected status badge (top-right), and a summary strip above the grid showing total channels and how many are live (e.g. '5 channels · 5 connected' with a green pulse dot)."},
      {label:"Per-card stats row — Users, Groups, Requests",status:"planned",desc:"Each card contains a three-cell stats row: Users (unique users who have messaged the bot), Groups (group chats the bot is active in — 'Groups unsupported' shown for platforms like WeChat that don't allow bots in groups), and Requests (messages currently pending or in-flight). Each stat is in its own bordered box — label above, number below in larger weight. Updates live via webhook or API polling."},
      {label:"Assigned AI Agent row",status:"planned",desc:"Below the stats row, an agent row shows the assigned AI Agent's logo, name, truncated one-line description, and a right-pointing chevron. Clicking navigates directly to that agent's configuration page. Makes the agent–channel relationship visible at a glance without opening Settings."},
      {label:"Channel action buttons — Settings, Disconnect, Re-bind, Unbind",status:"planned",desc:"Two buttons at the bottom of each card. Most platforms: Settings (gear icon, opens configuration panel) and Disconnect (power icon, revokes connection). OAuth platforms like WeChat: Re-bind (re-launches OAuth, preserves stats and agent assignments) and Unbind (fully removes connection and wipes data). Re-bind refreshes credentials; Unbind starts over."},
      {label:"Connect a new channel",status:"planned",desc:"A 'Connect Channel' button opens a stepped modal: (1) select platform — Telegram, Discord, WhatsApp, WeChat, DingTalk, Feishu, Slack, and more; (2) enter bot token or complete OAuth flow; (3) test with a live ping; (4) assign an AI Agent. Once connected, the new card appears with a Connected badge. All credentials stored locally — no cloud relay required."},
      {label:"Per-channel configuration panel",status:"planned",desc:"Each channel's Settings panel: bot token or API key (masked, show/hide toggle), webhook URL (auto-generated, copyable), user/group whitelists, message rate limit (messages per minute), response language preference, and assigned AI Agent (switchable without disconnecting). A 'Test connection' button sends a live ping to verify the channel. Changes save on blur."}
    ]},
    {area:"Connectors",color:"green",items:[
      {label:"Connectors directory — browse, search, filter by category",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user works across many tools and wants Plenix to consolidate all their data in one place. The Connectors screen is a searchable, filterable directory of every supported integration. A search bar filters by name. Category tabs let the user browse by type: All, AI & Search, Collaboration & Communication, Productivity & Project Management, Social & Media, Developer Tools, and Sales & Marketing. A summary strip shows total connectors and how many are live. A Refresh button re-checks all statuses. Each connector is a card showing the platform logo, name, a one-line capability description, and a status badge — Connected (green, Disconnect button), Not Connected (gray, + Connect button), or Coming Soon (dimmed, non-interactive). Connectors are data bridges that bring information from other platforms into Plenix for the user to work with directly. AI is available as an assistant. This is distinct from Channels, which are outbound messaging platforms for AI agents."},
      {label:"Collaboration & Communication connectors",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: All the user's communication — across multiple platforms — accessible from Plenix's Messaging and Email sections without context-switching. Documented connectors: Gmail — full mailbox, read/draft/send, labels, search; inbox appears in Plenix Email section. Microsoft Teams — read channels, post updates, access meeting transcripts; appears in Messaging. Outlook — email + calendar sync for Microsoft accounts. Slack — read and post to channels, DMs, message search; threads appear in Messaging alongside other channels. Asana — read projects and tasks, create and update tasks from Plenix's Tasks section; changes sync back to Asana. All use OAuth 2.0 with credentials stored locally."},
      {label:"Productivity & Project Management connectors",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user's planning and tracking tools reflected inside Plenix without duplicating data entry. Documented connectors: Google Calendar — two-way sync; events appear in Plenix's Calendar section and can push back. Google Tasks — read and create tasks; appear in Plenix Tasks with a source badge. Google Docs — linked docs appear in project Files tabs. Google Sheets — read sheet data into Plenix (supplier lists, budgets, inventory); can power Automation report tasks. Notion — read pages and databases; entries appear as tasks, notes, or project records. All support real-time or scheduled sync depending on API capabilities."},
      {label:"Social & Media connectors",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Social and community engagement data visible inside Plenix without logging into each platform. Documented connectors: Discord — read and post to guild channels; community activity appears in Messaging. YouTube — channel analytics (views, watch time, subscribers), video metadata, comment monitoring. Reddit — subreddit monitoring for mentions, trending discussions, keyword alerts; surfaces in the monitoring feed. Data is read-only by default; write permissions require explicit opt-in during the connect flow."},
      {label:"Developer Tools connectors",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Code activity and web research integrated with project management and notes. GitHub — repositories, issues, pull requests, commit history; issues sync to Tasks, commits appear in project activity feeds, PRs can trigger automation tasks. Firecrawl — scrape and extract structured data from any URL for use by Automation tasks (Competitor Watch, Tech Radar). Firecrawl outputs are delivered as notes or feed items, not raw HTML."},
      {label:"Sales & Marketing connectors",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Consolidated marketing and ecommerce reporting inside Plenix. Documented connectors: SEMrush — domain analytics, keyword rankings, backlink data, competitor comparisons. Google Analytics — GA4 sessions, conversion events, channel performance; weekly reports automatable. Google Ads — campaign performance, wasted spend identification, cost-per-conversion. Google Search Console — search performance, indexed pages, crawl errors. Shopify — orders, inventory, product data, customer records; order data can trigger task creation. eBay — listings, sales, offers, seller performance metrics. Meta Ads Manager — ad insights and optimization reports."},
      {label:"AI & Search connectors",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Plenix's AI assistant needs access to real-time web information beyond the user's own data. Perplexity Search — gives the AI assistant real-time web search with citations, enabling answers about current events, recent releases, and competitor news post-dating training data. Planned additions: direct model provider APIs (Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini) for configuring which model powers the AI assistant; specialized search APIs for procurement (customs data, trade directory search) and legal/compliance research."},
      {label:"Connector data routing into Plenix sections",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Connecting a service makes its data appear in the correct Plenix section automatically — no manual import needed. Routing rules: Gmail/Outlook → Email section. Google Calendar/Outlook Calendar → Calendar section. Asana/Google Tasks → Tasks section (with source badge). Google Docs/Notion → Notes or Files (user configures). GitHub issues → Tasks. GitHub repos → Files. Shopify/eBay → Commerce dashboard (planned). Social connectors → Social feed (planned). SEMrush/Analytics/Ads → Marketing dashboard (planned). All data shows a source badge so the user always knows whether a task came from Asana, Google Tasks, or was created natively."},
      {label:"OAuth, token management, and permission scopes",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user needs to connect services securely, understand exactly what Plenix can access, and revoke or narrow that access at any time. OAuth services open the provider's authorization page in a popup; the user grants scopes and is redirected back; the access token is stored in local encrypted storage. Token-based services prompt the user to paste their API key. A 'Manage permissions' view shows exact scopes granted (e.g. 'gmail.readonly, gmail.send') and allows re-authorizing with different scopes. Disconnecting revokes the token and removes all cached data. No credentials are ever transmitted to a Plenix server."}
    ]},
    {area:"Dashboard",color:"green",items:[
      {label:"3-column × 2-row CSS grid",status:"done",desc:"The dashboard uses a single CSS grid with gridTemplateColumns: 2fr 1fr 1fr and gridAutoRows: auto. This means the left column is twice as wide as each of the right two columns. Critically, every cell in a grid row is exactly as tall as the tallest widget in that row — not taller, not shorter. This was the key fix that replaced three separate flex columns which caused widgets to stretch vertically to fill all available space."},
      {label:"6 core widgets",status:"done",desc:"Projects (top-left, wide), Emails (top-center), Calendar (top-right), Tasks (bottom-left, wide), Notes (bottom-center), and Contacts (bottom-right). Each widget has a draggable handle bar at its top, a header row with a title and View All link, and a scrollable content area sized to its content rather than the window height."},
      {label:"Drag-and-drop widget rearrangement",status:"done",desc:"Each widget card is draggable. Dragging a widget over another and dropping swaps their positions in the layout order array. The drag target is highlighted with a blue top bar and blue box shadow. The layout order is stored in state and persists for the session."},
      {label:"Live clock strip",status:"done",desc:"A compact strip above the widgets shows the current time (12h format, updates every second) and the full date. Rendered in the monospace font for alignment precision. A thin vertical rule separates time from date."},
      {label:"Projects widget card view",status:"done",desc:"Project cards in a 2-column grid with gridAutoRows: max-content. Each card has a 3px left border in the project color, the project name tinted in that color, and a subtle color wash on hover."},
      {label:"Contacts widget",status:"done",desc:"Horizontal contact rows — avatar, name, message icon (blue), call icon (green). Icons use stopPropagation and have hover states."},
      {label:"Tasks widget — today, overdue, and upcoming",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The most important thing on the dashboard is knowing what needs to be done today without opening the Tasks section. The Tasks widget shows a consolidated, prioritized list covering three scopes: Today (tasks due today), Overdue (tasks past their due date, shown with a red date indicator), and Upcoming (tasks due in the next 7 days). Each scope is a labeled sub-section within the widget. Tasks are ordered by priority within each scope. Each row shows the checkbox, priority dot, task title, and due date. Checking a task from the widget marks it complete in the Tasks section in real time. The widget respects the task settings (show/hide priority dot, show/hide project label). A count badge on the widget header shows total incomplete items across all three scopes."},
      {label:"Active timer widget — running session visibility",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: When the user starts a focus session (Pomodoro or manual timer) from a task, the active timer should be visible from the dashboard without navigating away. The Active Timer widget shows: the current task being worked on, the elapsed time for the session (counting up) or remaining time (counting down for Pomodoro), a pause/resume button, and a stop/complete button. If no timer is running, the widget shows a prompt to start a focus session and a list of the top 3 priority tasks as quick-start options. The timer state is shared with the Calendar timeline view — starting a timer from the dashboard marks that time block as In Progress on the timeline."},
      {label:"Habit tracking widget — streaks and daily completion",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user tracks daily habits (exercise, reading, meditation, water intake, etc.) and wants to see and update their habit status from the dashboard without opening a separate Habits section. The Habits widget shows each tracked habit as a row with: the habit name, an icon, a check button (tap to mark today's habit complete), and a streak counter (consecutive days completed). Completed habits are shown with a green checkmark and a faded appearance so uncompleted ones stand out. The streak number updates immediately when a habit is checked. A mini progress bar shows how many habits have been completed today vs total (e.g. 3/5). Habits are configured in Settings → Habits with name, icon, color, and target frequency. This widget is the daily entry point for habit logging — not a full habit analytics view."},
      {label:"Calendar events widget — today's schedule inline",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user needs to see today's calendar events from the dashboard without opening the Calendar section. The Calendar widget shows: a compact timeline of today's events (title, time, duration as a colored bar), the next upcoming event highlighted at the top, and events color-coded by their source calendar. All-day events appear at the top of the list. The widget reads from all connected calendar providers and the user's Plenix time blocks simultaneously, so it is a unified view of committed time. Clicking any event opens the full event detail (and its linked note if one exists). A 'Join' button appears on events with a detected video conference link (Zoom, Meet, Teams). The widget updates in real time as connected calendar providers push new events."},
      {label:"Notes widget — recent notes with project context",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants quick access to recently edited notes without navigating to the Notes section — especially notes tied to active projects. The Notes widget shows the most recently created or modified notes as compact rows: note title, folder, a one-line preview, and timestamp. If a note is linked to a project, the project's color dot and name appear as a badge. Clicking a note row opens it in the Notes section. A 'New note' quick-add button at the top of the widget creates a new note and navigates to the editor. The number of notes shown is configurable (3, 5, or 7 rows). This gives the user a contextual anchor to ongoing work — seeing a project note surfaces the context of what they were working on last."},
      {label:"Shopping list widget — manage list from dashboard",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user maintains a shopping list as part of their daily life and wants to add items and check things off without navigating anywhere. The Shopping List widget is a lightweight checklist embedded directly in the dashboard. Key design principle: speed and simplicity above all — no categories, no tags, no labels, no extra structure. Item entry: a text input at the top of the widget accepts a new item name; pressing Enter adds it instantly and it appears at the top of the list for immediate visibility. Items are displayed in a single continuous list with no sub-sections. Completion: each item has a checkbox; checking it applies a strikethrough state and removes the item from the active list after a brief animation (rather than staying in a completed pile). Reordering: items can be dragged up and down to match store layout or shopping priority — the new order saves immediately with no manual save step. The order persists across sessions. The list is available as a full-screen view in the Tasks section (under a dedicated Shopping List area) and as a dashboard widget. Data is the same underlying list in both places — the widget is a shortcut, not a separate store. Cloud sync keeps the list current across devices (useful when one device is at home making the list and another is at the store checking items off)."},
      {label:"Summary metrics cards — system-wide counts at a glance",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants a quick health check of their whole system — how many projects are active, how many tasks are outstanding, whether they are on top of their notes and habits — without drilling into each section. The dashboard includes a row of summary metric cards (KPI-style), each showing a count and a label: Total projects, Active tasks, Completed tasks today, Total notes, Calendar events today, Tracked habits, and Current habit streak (longest active streak in days). Each card is clickable — clicking 'Active tasks' navigates to the Tasks section filtered to incomplete items. Metric cards update in real time. A subtle trend indicator (up/down arrow) shows whether each metric has increased or decreased since yesterday, giving the user a sense of momentum."},
      {label:"Weather widget",status:"planned",desc:"Sits in the dashboard as a lightweight status strip. Shows current weather conditions (icon + short description) and temperature. Configurable: actual vs feels-like temperature, Celsius vs Fahrenheit. Auto-refreshes every 5 minutes. Location determined via IP-based geolocation — the user can tap the location label to verify the inferred city. Enabled/disabled in Settings → Dashboard."},
      {label:"Widget settings — per-widget configuration",status:"done",desc:"Each widget type has its own configurable fields accessible from the dashboard Settings panel: density (card vs list view for Projects), visible fields (show/hide category, status, file count, tags, description), filtering (include/exclude archived or paused projects), and max item count. Changes apply immediately without reloading."}
    ]},
    {area:"Documents",color:"gray",items:[
      {label:"Long-form document editor — distinct from Notes",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Notes are for quick captures, meeting summaries, and short-form reference. Documents are for structured long-form content: SOPs (standard operating procedures), project briefs, product specs, reports, proposals, onboarding guides, client deliverables. The Documents section uses the same rich-text editor as Notes but with additions appropriate for longer content: a document outline panel (H1/H2/H3 headings auto-populate a clickable table of contents in the sidebar), page-like layout with configurable margins and line width, multi-page awareness for PDF export, tables (not in Notes), and image embedding. Documents are separate from Notes in the sidebar and search filters — the user always knows whether they are working on a quick note or a formal document."},
      {label:"Rich-text editor — headings, tables, images, code blocks",status:"planned",desc:"The document editor supports all Note formatting (H1–H3, bold, italic, underline, lists, checklists, blockquotes, code blocks with syntax highlighting) plus: Tables (insert table, add/remove rows and columns, merge cells, set column alignment), Images (drag-and-drop from local files or paste from clipboard; images are stored in Plenix's file storage and embedded as references, not base64 blobs), Horizontal dividers, and Callout blocks (a highlighted box for warnings, tips, or important notices — similar to Notion's callout block). The editor has a floating toolbar that appears when text is selected, plus a persistent toolbar at the top for block-level actions (insert table, insert image, etc.)."},
      {label:"Auto-save with word count",status:"planned",desc:"Documents auto-save using the same debounce mechanism as Notes (500ms after last keystroke). Save status is shown in the document header: Editing → Saving... → Saved [timestamp]. Word count is tracked per document and shown persistently in the bottom status bar — total words, selected words (if text is selected), and character count. For long documents, a reading time estimate is also shown (based on 200 words per minute average reading speed). The document title is editable inline in the header — clicking it activates an edit mode."},
      {label:"Folder organization with drag-and-drop",status:"planned",desc:"Documents are organized into folders with unlimited nesting depth. The folder structure appears in the Documents sidebar as a collapsible tree. New folders are created from a right-click context menu or a + button at the top of the sidebar. Documents are moved between folders by dragging them in the sidebar tree. Folders can be renamed and deleted (with a confirmation when the folder contains documents). A Favorites section at the top of the sidebar shows pinned documents for quick access. A Recent section shows the 5 most recently opened documents regardless of their folder location."},
      {label:"PDF export with formatted layout",status:"planned",desc:"Any document can be exported as a PDF. The export engine renders the document with proper page breaks, consistent typography, page numbers (in the footer), and the document title on the first page. Heading levels determine the visual hierarchy in the export — H1 is the largest, H3 the smallest. Tables render with borders. Code blocks use a monospace font with a light gray background. Images are embedded at their natural aspect ratio, scaling to fit the page width. The PDF output is suitable for sharing with clients or archiving as a formal document. A Print action uses the same rendering pipeline as PDF export."},
      {label:"External storage integration — OneDrive, Google Drive, Dropbox",status:"planned",desc:"Documents can be linked to files stored in external cloud storage providers. Connecting OneDrive, Google Drive, or Dropbox via the Connectors section makes those files accessible from within Plenix's Documents section — they appear as linked documents in the folder tree with a cloud badge indicating their source. Clicking a linked document opens it in the external provider's viewer within a panel, or downloads it for local editing. Changes to externally linked files sync back to the provider on save. This allows the user to manage all their documents — both Plenix-native and externally stored — from a single interface without duplicating files."},
      {label:"Full-text search across all documents",status:"planned",desc:"The Documents search bar queries the full text of all documents — not just titles. Results show the document title, the folder it is in, a snippet of the matching content with the search term highlighted, and the last modified date. Search spans across all folders simultaneously. Advanced filters: by folder, by date range, by project association, by whether the document has been exported. Search is also accessible from the global header search bar, which returns document results alongside notes, tasks, and contacts."},
      {label:"Project association and integration",status:"planned",desc:"Documents can be linked to a project — appearing in that project's Files tab alongside uploaded files and linked notes. A document linked to a project inherits the project's color accent in the folder tree so it is visually identifiable. Documents can also be linked to tasks (the document is the deliverable for the task), calendar events (meeting brief or post-meeting minutes), and contacts (a proposal for a specific client). The Related section at the bottom of each open document shows all linked items so the user can navigate between the document and its context without leaving the editor."}
    ]},
    {area:"Email Client",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Native IMAP/SMTP email client — built into Plenix",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user does not want to switch between Plenix and a separate email app (Apple Mail, Thunderbird, Spark, Outlook) to handle their inbox. Plenix includes a full email client built directly into the app — not a webview of Gmail or Outlook, but a native IMAP/SMTP client that connects directly to any email provider. The user connects their account(s) by entering IMAP server, IMAP port, SMTP server, SMTP port, username, and password (or OAuth for Google/Microsoft accounts). Plenix fetches mail via IMAP and sends via SMTP. Multiple accounts can be connected simultaneously. All email stays in sync with the provider — actions taken in Plenix (read, archive, delete, move) are reflected on the server and in any other mail client the user has connected. The email client lives inside Plenix so it shares context with tasks, projects, calendar, contacts, and notes — no duplicate data, no context switching."},
      {label:"Folder browsing and mailbox navigation",status:"planned",desc:"The Email sidebar shows all IMAP folders for each connected account: Inbox, Sent, Drafts, Trash, Spam, and any custom folders or labels the user has created in their mail provider. Folders are listed under a collapsible account header (useful when multiple accounts are connected). Clicking a folder loads its messages in the main panel. Unread counts are shown on folder rows. IMAP folder sync is real-time for providers that support IMAP IDLE (Gmail, Fastmail, most modern providers); otherwise it polls every 2 minutes. The folder list refreshes automatically when new mail arrives."},
      {label:"Email composition — rich text, reply, forward, attachments",status:"planned",desc:"The compose panel supports: rich-text body (bold, italic, underline, inline links, bullet lists — same editor as Notes), To/CC/BCC fields with contact autocomplete (pulls from the Contacts section), Subject line, Attachments (drag-and-drop or file picker — files from the Files section can be attached directly without downloading), Reply (quotes the original message with attribution header), Reply All (adds all original recipients to CC), and Forward (pre-fills body with original message). Drafts are auto-saved to the IMAP Drafts folder every 30 seconds. A send delay option (configurable: 5, 10, 30 seconds) allows the user to undo a sent email before it is transmitted. Sent emails are stored in the IMAP Sent folder on the server."},
      {label:"Unified Inbox — multiple accounts in one view",status:"planned",desc:"When multiple email accounts are connected, the Unified Inbox aggregates all inboxes into a single chronological feed — the user sees all unread email from all accounts without switching between account views. Each message shows a small account badge (colored by account) so the user always knows which account it belongs to. Replying from the Unified Inbox automatically uses the correct From address for that account. The Unified Inbox is distinct from but complementary to the broader Unified Inbox feature (which also includes WhatsApp, social mentions, etc.) — the email Unified Inbox is an email-only view within the Email section."},
      {label:"Search across the full mailbox",status:"planned",desc:"Search in the Email section queries the full mailbox — subject, sender, recipient, and body text — across all connected accounts. Search is executed against both locally cached messages and the IMAP server (via IMAP SEARCH command) so older messages not yet downloaded are also findable. Results show the matching messages with the search term highlighted. Filters can be applied to the search results: by account, by date range, by folder, by read/unread status, and by whether the message has attachments. Search results are sorted by date (most recent first) by default."},
      {label:"Read/unread flags and message management",status:"planned",desc:"Marking a message as read or unread updates the IMAP Seen flag on the server — the change is reflected in all clients. Other per-message actions: Archive (moves to the provider's archive folder), Delete (moves to Trash — permanent deletion requires emptying Trash or a second delete action), Move to folder (folder picker dropdown), Star/Flag (sets the IMAP Flagged flag), and Mark as spam (moves to the Spam folder and reports to the provider if the provider supports feedback). Bulk actions: select multiple messages and apply any of the above actions simultaneously. A swipe gesture on mobile performs configurable quick actions (e.g. swipe right = archive, swipe left = delete)."},
      {label:"Email linked to contacts, tasks, and projects",status:"planned",desc:"Every email from a known contact (a sender whose email address matches a record in the Contacts section) shows that contact's profile card in the right panel when the email is open — name, company, role, tags, interaction history, and linked tasks. From any open email, the user can: Create a task linked to this email (the email subject becomes the task title, the email body becomes the task notes, the sender is linked as the contact), Save as a note (useful for email threads that contain reference information), Add to a project (link the email thread to a project so it appears in that project's activity feed). This turns the email client into a lightweight CRM layer — every inbound email is an opportunity to capture context without manually copying information."}
    ]},
    {area:"Files",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"File table with columns",status:"done",desc:"The Files screen shows all files in a structured table with five columns: Name (with emoji type icon and AI badge where applicable), Type (tag pill), Size (monospace), Modified date, and Folder. Column headers use uppercase labels. Rows highlight on hover and the table is scrollable. The Upload button in the top-right opens the system file picker."},
      {label:"AI badge on AI-named files",status:"done",desc:"Files whose names were generated by the AI File Naming feature (via Capture) are tagged with a small blue 'AI' badge next to their name. The name itself is also rendered in the accent dark blue rather than the default text color, making AI-named files immediately distinguishable. This badge can be toggled off in Settings → Files."}
    ]},
    {area:"Finance Planner",color:"brown",items:[
      {label:"Income and expense tracking",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to track their personal or business finances — income, recurring expenses, one-off purchases, and overall budget health — without using a separate app. The Finance Planner allows the user to log income and expense transactions with: amount, date, category (user-defined — e.g. Rent, Utilities, Salary, Freelance income, Travel), description, and a Paid/Pending status. Transactions can be marked as recurring (with a frequency and end date) so regular items like rent, subscriptions, and salary auto-populate without manual re-entry each period. The transaction list is filterable by date range, category, and type (income vs expense)."},
      {label:"Budget planning and category limits",status:"planned",desc:"The user can define a budget — a monthly allocation per category (e.g. Groceries: £400, Entertainment: £100, Utilities: £150). The Finance Planner compares actual spending to the budget in real time, showing a progress bar per category that fills as spending accumulates. Categories over budget are highlighted in red. The budget view shows total income, total expenses, and the net surplus or deficit for the period. Budget limits can be set independently for each month, allowing for variable months (e.g. higher entertainment budget in December)."},
      {label:"Reporting and trends",status:"planned",desc:"The Finance Planner reports view shows: monthly totals (income and expenses by month, as a bar chart), category breakdown (pie chart of expense distribution), year-to-date summary, and trend lines for key categories over time. The user can compare any two months side by side. An export function downloads transactions as a CSV for use in a spreadsheet or tax preparation tool. The Finance Planner is personal/small business in scope — it is not a full accounting system but a clear, fast tool for tracking where money goes."}
    ]},
    {area:"Goal Tracker",color:"purple",items:[
      {label:"Goal definition and progress tracking",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to track progress toward medium and long-term objectives — not just individual tasks, but outcome-level goals like 'Launch the new product line by Q3', 'Read 24 books this year', 'Reach 10kg weight loss by June'. Goals are defined with: a title, a description of the desired outcome, a target date, a measurement type (binary complete/incomplete, numerical target with a current value and unit, or percentage-based), and a review cadence (weekly, monthly). The Goal Tracker shows all active goals with their current progress visually represented as a progress bar or numerical gauge. Goals can be linked to projects (the project is the vehicle for achieving the goal), tasks (specific milestones toward the goal), and habits (recurring behaviors that support the goal)."},
      {label:"Goal review cadence and weekly check-in",status:"planned",desc:"Each goal has a configurable review cadence. When a review is due, the user is prompted to: update the goal's current progress value, write a brief reflection note (what is going well, what is blocked), and optionally adjust the target date or measurement. Review notes are stored in a timeline on the goal's detail page — building a running journal of the goal's progress over time. The Weekly Review automation in the Automations section can automatically generate a goal progress summary every week, pulling the latest values from all active goals and presenting them in a structured note."}
    ]},
    {area:"Habit Tracker",color:"green",items:[
      {label:"Daily habit logging and streaks",status:"planned",desc:"The user defines habits with a name, icon, color, and target frequency (daily, specific days, or X times per week). Each habit has a daily check-in view with a checkbox. Completing increments the streak counter. Missing a day breaks the streak. Streaks shown prominently as motivation. The Habits widget on the dashboard allows one-tap daily logging. Historical data preserved from creation date."},
      {label:"Habit analytics — completion rates and trends",status:"planned",desc:"Analytics view shows: completion rate per habit (percentage of days completed over a configurable period), streak history (longest, current, average streak), weekly and monthly heatmap colored by completion (similar to GitHub contribution graph), and comparison between habits to identify which are sticky vs being neglected."}
    ]},
    {area:"Journal",color:"gray",items:[
      {label:"Daily journal entries with prompts and templates",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants a private space for daily reflection — what happened, how they felt, what they are grateful for, what they want to focus on tomorrow. The Journal section provides a chronological log of dated entries. Each entry can be free-form (open text) or guided by a template (e.g. Morning Pages: 3 things I am grateful for, 1 intention for the day, 1 thing I am looking forward to; Evening Reflection: What went well, What was challenging, What I learned). Templates are user-defined with named sections. Prompts can be configured to appear automatically at set times (e.g. a morning prompt at 8 AM, an evening prompt at 9 PM). Journal entries support the same rich-text formatting as Notes."},
      {label:"People and event linking",status:"planned",desc:"Journal entries can be linked to contacts from the People section — the entry appears in that person's interaction history. Entries can also be linked to calendar events (post-meeting reflection), projects (project retrospective), habits (reflection on habit progress), and goals (notes on progress toward a goal)."}
    ]},
    {area:"Knowledge Graph",color:"purple",items:[
      {label:"Block-based outliner — every bullet is a first-class object",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Traditional notes treat the page as the atomic unit — you write a note, then link notes together. A block-based outliner treats every individual bullet point as a discrete, addressable object. In Plenix's Knowledge Graph mode, every bullet can be: referenced from anywhere else in the system (embed a specific bullet in another note without duplicating it), linked to (a backlink is automatically created on the source block), tagged, queried, and zoomed into (click any bullet to treat it as its own page with its own children). This structure means a note about a supplier can reference a specific paragraph from a market research note, a task, and a journal entry simultaneously — and all three links show up as backlinks on the original block. The outliner is hierarchical (parent–child indentation) and collapsible — parent blocks can be expanded to show children or collapsed to a single line for a high-level view. This is the Logseq / Roam Research model applied natively within Plenix."},
      {label:"Bidirectional links — automatic backlinks with [[double brackets]]",status:"planned",desc:"Typing [[page name]] anywhere in a note or block creates a link to that page and simultaneously creates a backlink on the target page. The backlink is automatic — the user does not need to go to the target page and manually add a reference back. Every page in Plenix's knowledge graph has a Linked References section at the bottom showing every block across the entire system that references it. This is the defining feature of networked note-taking: ideas connect in both directions, so the user can navigate from a concept to every place it appears across their notes, tasks, projects, and journal — not just from the source to the target. Block-level linking is also supported: [[page name#block-id]] links to a specific bullet within a page, enabling fine-grained cross-referencing."},
      {label:"Graph view — visual knowledge map",status:"planned",desc:"The Graph view renders every page and block in the knowledge base as a node, with links between them as edges. The result is an interactive visual map of how ideas connect. Nodes are sized by the number of connections (more-linked nodes appear larger). The user can click any node to navigate to that page. Filters allow the user to show/hide nodes by tag, by date range, or by connection count — useful for decluttering a large graph. The graph reveals patterns that are invisible in a folder-based system: clusters of tightly interconnected notes (a dense knowledge area), orphaned nodes (notes with no links — candidates for deletion or integration), and bridge nodes (notes that connect otherwise separate clusters). The graph is a diagnostic and navigation tool, not just a visual novelty."},
      {label:"Daily journal — chronological capture with automatic date pages",status:"planned",desc:"Every day, Logseq automatically opens a fresh daily page dated to today. Plenix's Knowledge Graph mode replicates this: a dedicated Journal section creates a new dated page each day (e.g. 'March 5, 2026') where the user can write anything — a free-form brain dump, a meeting note, a quick task, an idea. Because everything written on the daily page is a block, any bullet can be tagged with [[page name]] to link it to a topic page. The next time the user opens the topic page, that daily journal entry appears in its backlinks. Over time, the daily journal becomes a searchable, cross-referenced archive of thinking — not just a diary. The user can navigate back through any previous day. This combines the benefits of chronological journaling (easy capture) with the benefits of a knowledge graph (retroactive organization through links)."},
      {label:"Block references and block embeds",status:"planned",desc:"A block reference inserts a read-only copy of a specific block (bullet) from anywhere in the system into the current page. It looks like a quoted excerpt but it is live — if the original block is edited, the reference updates everywhere it is embedded. A block embed is similar but renders the block's full content including all its children (a sub-outline). Use cases: referencing a supplier's key specification in both a project plan and a purchasing note without duplicating it; embedding a meeting action items block in both the meeting note and the relevant project page; referencing a specific task's status in a weekly review note. Block references are created with ((block-id)) syntax. All block references are tracked — the original block's backlinks show every place it has been referenced or embedded."},
      {label:"Datalog-style queries — filter and surface content across the graph",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to answer questions like 'show me all blocks tagged #supplier that were created this week', or 'list all tasks that are TODO and linked to the Kitchen project'. Plenix's Knowledge Graph supports structured queries that filter across all blocks in the system using a Datalog-inspired syntax. Queries can filter by: page name or tag, block property values, creation or modification date, task status, and link relationships. The result is a live view — a dynamic table or list that updates automatically as new matching content is created. Queries are embedded directly in any note page as a block, so a project page can contain a live query showing all currently open tasks linked to that project. This transforms the knowledge base into a queryable database without requiring the user to manually maintain lists or dashboards."},
      {label:"PDF annotation — highlight and link directly to notes",status:"planned",desc:"PDFs can be dragged into the knowledge base and opened in a built-in PDF viewer. The user can highlight any text passage in the PDF — the highlight is saved as a block in the associated note, including: the highlighted text, the page number, and a link back to the exact position in the PDF. Clicking the link in the note jumps back to that location in the PDF. This makes Plenix's Knowledge Graph an academic-research-grade tool: the user can read a supplier specification, highlight the key technical requirements, and have those highlights automatically appear as linked blocks in their project notes. All annotations are stored locally alongside the PDF and are fully searchable. Zotero integration (for academic users) can import citation metadata alongside PDFs."},
      {label:"Flashcards with spaced repetition",status:"planned",desc:"Any block in the knowledge base can be converted to a flashcard with a single action. The front of the card is the block content; the back is its children (the answer or explanation). Flashcards use a spaced repetition algorithm (similar to Anki's SM-2) to schedule reviews — cards the user answers correctly are shown less frequently, cards answered incorrectly are shown more often. A daily review session surfaces the day's due cards. The review UI is minimal: front of card shown, user self-rates their recall (Again / Hard / Good / Easy), the algorithm schedules the next review. Flashcards remain linked to their source page — studying a flashcard is one click away from its full context in the knowledge graph. This makes the flashcard system genuinely useful for any domain: vocabulary, supplier specifications, product knowledge, regulatory requirements."},
      {label:"SQLite DB version — performance for large knowledge bases",status:"planned",desc:"The original file-based knowledge graph (storing notes as plain Markdown files) has a practical performance ceiling — graphs with more than 2,000 pages experience slow load times. Logseq addressed this with a SQLite-backed DB version in 2025–2026. Plenix's Knowledge Graph uses SQLite storage from the start, avoiding the performance ceiling entirely. The trade-off Logseq faced (abandoning portable plain-text files) is handled differently in Plenix: the knowledge graph data is stored in SQLite for performance, but a Markdown export function generates a portable plain-text version of the entire graph on demand. This gives the user both performance (SQLite) and portability (Markdown export) without choosing between them. Real-time collaboration (RTC) is possible with the database backend in a way that file-based systems cannot support."},
      {label:"Whiteboard — spatial canvas linked to the knowledge graph",status:"planned",desc:"The Whiteboard is a free-form canvas where blocks, pages, and new content can be arranged spatially. Unlike standalone whiteboard tools, Plenix's whiteboard is deeply integrated with the knowledge graph — blocks placed on the canvas remain connected to their original locations, changes sync bidirectionally, and entire pages can be embedded as cards on the canvas. Use cases: visualizing the structure of a complex project by dragging related pages and blocks into clusters; mapping relationships between suppliers, products, and certifications; creating a presentation layout while maintaining live links to the underlying notes. The canvas supports: text boxes (new blocks created directly on canvas), sticky notes, shapes, arrows with labels, and image embeds. The whiteboard is an alternative view into the knowledge graph, not a separate silo."},
      {label:"Plugin ecosystem — extend with community and custom plugins",status:"planned",desc:"The knowledge graph supports a plugin API that allows community-developed and user-written plugins to extend functionality. Logseq's plugin ecosystem (on which this is inspired) includes plugins for: enhanced task management, Pomodoro timers, Zotero citation import, GitHub issue sync, custom themes, spaced repetition enhancements, and many workflow-specific tools. Plenix's plugin API exposes: block creation and editing, page creation and linking, UI injection (adding buttons, panels, and sidebar items), query execution, and connection to Plenix's other sections (create a task from a plugin, add a calendar event, look up a contact). Plugins are installed from a marketplace and sandboxed for security. Users can also write custom plugins in JavaScript using the documented API."}
    ]},
    {area:"Leads Manager",color:"red",items:[
      {label:"Lead pipeline — CRM-style deal tracking",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user manages sales leads, business development conversations, or client acquisition workflows and needs to track where each opportunity stands without using a full CRM system. The Leads Manager provides a Kanban-style pipeline with customizable stages (e.g. New Lead / Qualified / Proposal Sent / Negotiating / Won / Lost). Each lead is a card containing: company or person name, deal value, expected close date, assigned contact (linked to People section), associated project (if the deal leads to a project), notes, and activity log. Cards move between stages by dragging. A won/lost status on a lead updates the pipeline summary metrics. Leads can be created from the Contacts section (convert a contact to a lead) or from an incoming email (one-click create lead from email sender)."},
      {label:"Lead metrics and pipeline value",status:"planned",desc:"The Leads Manager shows summary metrics at the top of the pipeline: total number of open leads, total pipeline value (sum of deal values across all non-closed leads), weighted pipeline value (each lead's value multiplied by a win probability percentage set per stage), leads won this month, and leads lost this month. A conversion funnel visualization shows how many leads are in each stage and the percentage that advance from one stage to the next — useful for identifying where leads are dropping off in the process."}
    ]},
    {area:"Messaging",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Thread list with unread badges",status:"done",desc:"The messaging screen shows a vertical list of conversation threads on the left. Each thread row shows the contact's avatar (initial circle with a unique background color), their name, and an unread count badge in the accent color when there are unread messages. Clicking a thread opens the conversation panel on the right and marks it as the active thread."},
      {label:"Bubble message view",status:"done",desc:"The right panel shows the conversation as a bubble chat — sent messages on the right in blue, received on the left in a gray card. Each bubble shows the message text and a timestamp. The bottom of the panel has a text input with a Send button. Pressing Send or clicking the button appends a new bubble to the thread in the current session."},
      {label:"Channel sidebar",status:"done",desc:"The contextual sidebar for Messaging groups communication channels (SMS, WhatsApp, Email) at the top, followed by the individual thread list below. This lets users quickly filter to a specific channel type before drilling into a thread."}
    ]},
    {area:"Model Selection",color:"purple",items:[
      {label:"Multi-model support — choose the right AI for each task",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Different tasks need different AI capabilities. Writing a quick email requires a different model than generating a complex multi-file codebase or analyzing 500 pages of legal documents. Plenix gives the user access to multiple AI models from different providers — Anthropic (Claude), Google (Gemini), OpenAI (GPT), xAI (Grok), and others. The model selector is accessible from the chat input area. The selected model persists per thread, so the user can use Claude Opus for deep research in one thread and Claude Haiku for quick drafts in another. All models have equal access to the same tools: web search, file handling, data analysis, and all connected integrations."},
      {label:"Model catalog — context windows, capabilities, cost tiers",status:"planned",desc:"The model selector shows a comparison table or card list with: model name, provider, context window size, whether prompt caching is supported, cost tier (Economy / Standard / Premium), and a one-line 'best for' description. Documented models: Claude Opus 4.7 (Anthropic, 1M context, Premium — hardest software engineering and long-horizon agent work), Claude Opus 4.6 (Anthropic, 1M, Premium — previous flagship, complex reasoning), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, 1M, Standard — best balance of speed and intelligence), Claude Haiku 4.5 (Anthropic, 200K, Economy — fast and cost-efficient), Gemini 3.1 Pro (Google, 1M, Standard — advanced reasoning with multimodal input), GPT-5.4 (OpenAI, 1M, Standard — capable all-rounder with reasoning), MiniMax M2.7 (MiniMax, 196K, Economy — agentic coding at very low cost), Grok 4.20 (xAI, 2M context, Standard — largest available context window). This list grows as new models release."},
      {label:"Extended thinking toggle in model selector",status:"planned",desc:"When a thinking-capable model is selected (Claude Sonnet 4.6 or Claude Opus variants), a 'Max Thinking' toggle appears at the top of the model selector dropdown. Enabling it activates extended reasoning mode for that thread. The brain icon and toggle state are visually prominent so the user always knows whether extended thinking is active. The toggle is per-thread and defaults to off. Turning it on increases credit consumption per message."},
      {label:"Prompt caching — automatic cost and latency reduction",status:"planned",desc:"Most models support prompt caching, which reduces both cost and response latency on follow-up messages within the same thread. When the AI processes a message, repeated parts of the conversation (system prompt, earlier messages, uploaded files already seen) are served from cache at a lower token rate rather than re-processed in full. Caching is automatic — the user does not need to configure anything. The model selector card for each model shows whether caching is supported. For long research threads or multi-turn workflows, caching can meaningfully reduce credit consumption."},
      {label:"Credit system — usage tracking and cost transparency",status:"planned",desc:"Model usage is measured in credits deducted based on actual token consumption. Three cost tiers: Economy (~0.1–1 credits per message: MiniMax M2.7, Claude Haiku 4.5), Standard (~1–6 credits: Claude Sonnet 4.6, GPT-5.4, Gemini 3.1 Pro, Grok 4.20), Premium (~5–20 credits: Claude Opus 4.7, Claude Opus 4.6). Exact cost per message depends on message length, conversation history length, and output length. Remaining credits are displayed in the sidebar. A credit usage breakdown is available in Account settings showing spend by model, date range, and task type. Prompt caching lowers effective cost for long threads. Extended thinking increases cost for that message."}
    ]},
    {area:"Navigation",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Single-row header bar",status:"done",desc:"The entire navigation system — logo, section icons, search, and actions — collapses into a single 52px horizontal bar. This was a deliberate design decision to maximize vertical space for content. The bar is split into three segments using CSS Grid (1fr auto 1fr) so the search input is always mathematically centered regardless of how wide the left or right segments grow."},
      {label:"9 section icons with tooltips",status:"done",desc:"Home, Projects, Messages, Contacts, Calendar, Tasks, Notes, Bookmarks, and Files are each represented by an outline icon in the nav bar. Each icon shows a tooltip on hover. The active section is highlighted with the accent background color. Icons are rendered as inline SVG paths so they scale perfectly at any DPI without external font or image dependencies."},
      {label:"3-segment header layout",status:"done",desc:"Segment 1 (left): Plenix logo followed by section icons and a settings icon separated by a vertical rule. Segment 2 (center): search bar, always centered. Segment 3 (right): Capture button and user avatar. This structure ensures the search bar never shifts when the left nav grows or shrinks."},
      {label:"Header bar themes",status:"done",desc:"Three header styles are available and switchable in Settings → Appearance: Accent blue (Windows blue background, white icons — bold brand statement), White (clean minimal with shadow), and Frost (cool blue-gray gradient, slightly translucent). The active theme updates the bar background, icon colors, search input style, keyboard shortcut badge, and Capture button style all simultaneously."},
      {label:"Sidebar collapse & drag-resize",status:"done",desc:"Every contextual sidebar (Projects, Contacts, Calendar, etc.) can be collapsed to a 32px rail by clicking the chevron button in its top-right corner. It can also be resized by dragging the right edge anywhere between 140px and 320px wide. Both interactions use native mouse events with no external library. The resize handle highlights in the accent color on hover."},
      {label:"Section-specific sidebar content",status:"done",desc:"The left sidebar changes its content entirely based on which section is active. Projects shows a grouped project list. Calendar shows the calendars list and import option. Contacts shows groups. Tasks shows filters (All, Due soon, By Project). Notes shows folders. Files shows folders. Messaging shows channels and threads. Each sidebar is context-aware and shares the same collapse/resize infrastructure."}
    ]},
    {area:"Notes",color:"purple",items:[
      {label:"Two-pane notes layout",status:"done",desc:"Notes screen splits into a left list and a right preview pane. The list shows note title, first-line preview, folder label, tags, and date. Clicking a note renders its content in the right pane. Selected note is highlighted with the accent selection background."},
      {label:"Rich-text editor — headings, formatting, lists, code blocks",status:"planned",desc:"Notes are created using a rich-text editor supporting: Headings (H1–H3), Bold, italic, and underline, Ordered lists (numbered), Unordered lists (bulleted), Checklists (interactive checkboxes within the note body — useful for meeting agendas or packing lists), Blockquotes (indented quoted text with a left border), and Code blocks with syntax highlighting (identifies the language from the first line — JavaScript, Python, SQL, etc.). The editor is a contenteditable div with a formatting toolbar above it, replacing the plain textarea in the current implementation. All formatting is stored as structured JSON (similar to a ProseMirror document model) rather than raw HTML, making it portable and safe to render in different contexts."},
      {label:"Auto-save with status indicator",status:"planned",desc:"Notes are saved automatically during editing using a debounce mechanism — the save fires 500ms after the user stops typing, so rapid keystrokes do not trigger dozens of saves. A status indicator in the editor's top-right corner shows three states: Editing (unsaved changes present), Saving... (debounce fired, save in progress), and Saved (with a timestamp — 'Saved 2 minutes ago'). If the save fails (network error, storage error), the indicator shows an error state with a Retry button. Auto-save means the user never needs to press a Save button. The editor is always in a consistent saved state within 500ms of any change."},
      {label:"Pinned notes — quick access to important notes",status:"planned",desc:"Any note can be pinned. Pinned notes appear in a dedicated Pinned section at the top of the notes list, above all other notes, regardless of date or folder. The pin is toggled from the note's action menu (three-dot menu on the list row or in the editor toolbar). Multiple notes can be pinned simultaneously. Pinned notes are useful for: a current project's main reference note, a meeting template that is re-used frequently, a personal OKR or goal document that the user refers to daily. Unpinning moves the note back to its chronological position in the list."},
      {label:"Project association — notes linked to projects",status:"planned",desc:"Any note can be linked to a project. The link is set from a Project field in the note metadata panel (accessible from the editor's sidebar or a dropdown in the note action menu). Once linked: the note appears in the Project Detail's Notes tab alongside other project notes, a colored project badge appears on the note in the list view, and the note is findable by filtering the notes list by project. A note can only be linked to one project but can have multiple tags. Project-linked notes are also shown in the Project Overview mini widget on the dashboard. Notes created from calendar events (event-linked notes) are automatically linked to the event's associated project if one exists."},
      {label:"Full-text search across all notes",status:"planned",desc:"The search bar in the Notes section searches across the full text of every note — not just titles. Typing a word finds all notes where that word appears anywhere in the content. Search results are highlighted with the matching terms bolded in the preview. Results are ranked by recency (most recently edited first) with an option to rank by relevance (number of matches). Search is also accessible from the global header search bar, which returns note results alongside tasks, contacts, and files. For users with the browser extension installed, notes whose body text was captured from a web page are also searchable by URL."},
      {label:"Date-based grouping and chronological browsing",status:"planned",desc:"Notes in the list view are grouped by date: Today, Yesterday, This week, Last week, Earlier this month, and then by month for older notes. Within each group, notes are sorted by most recently modified. This makes it easy to find a note from a specific time period by scrolling rather than searching. The user can switch to Alphabetical sort (by title, A–Z), or Folder sort (grouped by folder/collection). Date grouping is the default because it mirrors how people actually remember notes — by when they were written, not by name."},
      {label:"Note editor toolbar and word count",status:"planned",desc:"A formatting toolbar appears above the editor with buttons for: Bold (Cmd/Ctrl+B), Italic (Cmd/Ctrl+I), Underline (Cmd/Ctrl+U), Heading level selector (H1/H2/H3), Bullet list, Numbered list, Checklist, Blockquote, Code block, Insert link, and Clear formatting. A live word count and character count sit in the bottom-right corner of the editor, updating with each keystroke. The toolbar is sticky — it stays visible when the note is long and the user scrolls down through the content."},
      {label:"AI note processing — raw input to structured output",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user types rough, unstructured notes during a meeting — fragmented sentences, bullet dumps, shorthand, half-finished thoughts — and wants them transformed into clean, readable documentation without manual reformatting. The AI Notepad mode accepts raw note content and processes it into structured output with a single action ('Clean up notes' button in the editor toolbar or a keyboard shortcut). The AI: identifies and separates different types of content (decisions made, action items, questions raised, context/background, next steps), applies consistent formatting (headings, bullets, bold for key names), removes filler words and redundancy, and preserves all substantive content without inventing anything that was not present in the raw input. The output is placed in the same note, replacing the raw content (with an undo option to restore the original), or in a new note linked to the original as a 'cleaned version'."},
      {label:"Meeting note templates — structured sections per meeting type",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Different meetings have different structures. A customer discovery call needs different sections than a one-on-one, a sprint planning session, or a supplier negotiation. Meeting templates define the section structure that the AI uses when processing notes. Built-in templates: Customer Discovery (sections: Customer background, Problem being solved, Current solution, Key pain points, Opportunities, Follow-ups), User Interview (Participant, Context, Questions asked, Key insights, Quotes, Action items), One-on-One (Wins since last meeting, Current blockers, Priorities this week, Career/growth discussion, Action items), Supplier Meeting (Supplier name and contact, Products discussed, Pricing discussed, Lead times, Sample requests, Follow-up actions), Sprint Planning (Goals for sprint, Stories committed, Dependencies, Risks, Action items), and Post-mortem (What went well, What went wrong, Root causes, Action items). The user selects a template before or after writing raw notes. The AI uses the template's section structure as the output schema. Templates are fully customizable — the user can rename sections, add new sections, reorder them, or create a template from scratch."},
      {label:"Action item extraction and task creation",status:"planned",desc:"After AI processing, the structured note contains an Action Items section listing every task that was identified in the raw notes. A 'Create tasks' button in the processed note converts each action item into a real task in the Tasks section, pre-filled with: the action item text as the task title, the meeting date as the creation date, and (if detectable from the text) a due date and an assignee linked to the People section. Each created task is linked back to the source note — the task's detail panel shows 'From meeting note: [note title]', and the note shows a 'Tasks created' badge listing the linked tasks. This closes the loop between note-taking and task management — action items from a meeting do not stay buried in notes; they become trackable tasks with one click."},
      {label:"Calendar event linking — meeting context in notes",status:"planned",desc:"When creating a new note from a calendar event (via the Event-linked notes feature in Calendar), the AI Notepad automatically pre-populates the note with context from the event: the meeting title, date and time, attendees (from the event's guest list, linked to contacts in the People section where they match), and the event description or agenda. This gives the note a structured starting point before the meeting begins. During or after the meeting, the user adds their raw notes below the pre-populated context. When the AI processes the note, it uses the attendee names and agenda topics as additional context to produce more accurate section headings and action item attributions (e.g. 'Marcus to send revised proposal by Friday' is attributed to Marcus's contact record rather than appearing as an anonymous action item)."},
      {label:"Consistent output for teams — shared templates and standardized formats",status:"planned",desc:"In team environments, inconsistent note formats create friction — meeting notes from different team members are structured differently, making it hard to scan, search, or share them. Shared meeting templates (stored in the Snippet Library's Teams section or a dedicated Templates library) allow a team to define a standard note format once and have all members use it. When a team member opens the AI Notepad and selects a shared template, their notes are processed into the same structure as everyone else's. A team's customer discovery notes always have the same sections, making it easy to compare insights across calls. The template owner can update the template and the change is immediately available to all team members — no redistribution required. Notes produced from a shared template display the template name as a badge so team members can identify the format at a glance."},
      {label:"Note integration — tasks, calendar, projects, journal",status:"planned",desc:"Notes can be bidirectionally linked to: Tasks (a note can be attached to a task as context; the task appears in the note's metadata as a linked task); Calendar events (notes created from events are linked back; a note icon appears on the event in calendar views); Projects (described above); and Journal entries (if the user journals, a note can be referenced from a journal entry, connecting a structured reference document with a personal reflection). These links are shown in a Related section at the bottom of each note, making it easy to navigate between connected content without losing context."}
    ]},
    {area:"Password Manager",color:"red",items:[
      {label:"Encrypted vault — logins, secure notes, credit cards",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user manages many passwords, API keys, secure notes, and payment details across their personal and professional life. Storing these in a browser password manager means they are tied to one browser and potentially synced through a third-party cloud the user does not control. Plenix includes a built-in encrypted vault that stores three types of records: Logins (username, password, URL, notes — the standard password entry), Secure notes (freeform encrypted text — for storing API keys, recovery codes, software license keys, or any text the user needs to keep private), and Credit cards (cardholder name, card number, expiry, CVV, billing address). Each record is an individual encrypted entry. The vault lives within Plenix but its contents are completely isolated — other Plenix features cannot read vault data without an explicit vault unlock action."},
      {label:"AES-256-GCM encryption with PBKDF2 key derivation",status:"planned",desc:"Vault data is encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM — the same standard used by 1Password, Bitwarden, and other leading password managers. The encryption key is derived from the user's master password using PBKDF2 (Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2) with a high iteration count (minimum 600,000 iterations as recommended by OWASP 2024) and a unique per-vault salt. The master password itself is never stored anywhere — neither locally nor on any server. The only way to decrypt the vault is to enter the correct master password. If the master password is forgotten, there is no recovery path (by design — this is the zero-knowledge guarantee). An optional recovery key (a long random string) can be generated at setup and stored offline for emergency access."},
      {label:"Auto-lock — configurable timeout and close-on-exit",status:"planned",desc:"The vault locks automatically after a configurable period of inactivity (options: 1 minute, 5 minutes, 15 minutes, 1 hour, Never). When locked, all vault content is hidden and the UI shows only the master password entry field. The vault also locks immediately when the application window is closed — it does not remain unlocked in memory when Plenix is not the active window (configurable: can be set to stay unlocked while the app is open). Re-authentication requires entering the master password. On supported devices, biometric authentication (Face ID, Touch ID, Windows Hello) can be used as a convenience unlock without requiring the full master password to be typed — but the biometric unlock only works after the master password has been entered at least once in the current session."},
      {label:"Password generator — configurable character sets and length",status:"planned",desc:"The vault includes a built-in password generator accessible from any login entry's password field and from a standalone generator tool in the vault. Configuration options: Length (8–128 characters, slider), Character sets (toggles for: Uppercase letters A–Z, Lowercase letters a–z, Numbers 0–9, Symbols !@#$%^&*() etc.), Exclude ambiguous characters (option to exclude characters that look similar — 0/O, 1/l/I — useful for passwords that must be read aloud), and Exclude specific characters (freeform exclusion list). The generated password is shown in a large, easily readable font. A Copy button copies it to the clipboard. A Regenerate button creates a new password with the same settings. Password strength is shown as a color-coded bar (Weak / Fair / Strong / Very Strong) calculated using entropy estimation."},
      {label:"One-click copy with automatic clipboard clearing",status:"planned",desc:"Any credential field (password, username, card number, CVV) can be copied with a single click — no need to reveal the field first. A toast notification confirms the copy. The clipboard is automatically cleared after a configurable timeout (default: 30 seconds, options: 10s, 30s, 60s, Never). When the clipboard clears, a second toast notification confirms it. This prevents sensitive data from remaining in the clipboard after the user has finished using it — reducing the risk of clipboard-reading malware or inadvertent pastes. On macOS, the clipboard clearing uses a system-level clipboard write to overwrite the sensitive content rather than relying on an API call that some apps can intercept."},
      {label:"Full-text search across vault items",status:"planned",desc:"The vault search bar queries across all item types simultaneously: login names, URLs, usernames, secure note titles and body text, and credit card cardholder names. The master password, actual passwords, card numbers, and CVVs are never included in the search index — only non-sensitive metadata is searchable. Searching 'amazon' finds a login entry named 'Amazon Shopping' and a secure note titled 'Amazon AWS keys'. Results are shown as a flat list with the item type icon (key for logins, lock for secure notes, card for credit cards). Tags can be added to vault items and are also searchable — useful for grouping items by context (Work, Personal, Client A, etc.)."}
    ]},
    {area:"People & Contacts",color:"red",items:[
      {label:"Contact list and detail panel — current implementation",status:"done",desc:"The Contacts screen shows a scrollable list of contacts on the left, each row showing an avatar circle, full name, role title, and company. Clicking a contact opens a right panel with more detail. The list supports group filtering via the sidebar (Clients, Contractors, Personal, Professional). The dashboard widget shows horizontal contact rows with a message icon and call icon on the right. This is the baseline — the features below describe the full planned system."},
      {label:"Relationship hierarchy — organizing people by closeness",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A flat alphabetical contact list treats a close friend the same as someone you met once at a conference. Plenix organizes people within a relationship-based hierarchy that reflects the strength and nature of each connection. The hierarchy tiers are: Family, Extended Family, Close Connections, Casual Connections, Acquaintances, Professional/Business Contacts, and Distant/Minimal Contact. Each tier is a collapsible section in the People sidebar. This structure gives the user an immediate overview of their network distribution — how many close vs casual connections they have — and makes it obvious where attention may be needed. Tiers can be filtered or hidden by context (e.g. show only Personal tiers when not in work mode). The user assigns each person to a tier and can move them as the relationship evolves over time."},
      {label:"Relationship focus — satisfactory vs needs attention",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Within any tier, some relationships are healthy and some have drifted. Each person can be marked with a relationship status: Satisfactory (things are good, no action needed) or Needs Attention (the relationship requires effort, more frequent contact, or a specific action). This single flag creates an actionable filtered view: 'Show me everyone who needs attention right now.' This is not a judgment about the person but a self-directed signal to the user about where to invest relational energy. The Needs Attention view is the most action-oriented view in the People section — it is the starting point for intentional relationship maintenance."},
      {label:"Reconnect tracking — intervals, last contact, next due",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Important relationships fade when contact becomes irregular. Plenix supports configurable reconnect intervals per person so the system surfaces reminders when it is time to reach out. For each person the user defines: a desired reconnect frequency (daily, weekly, fortnightly, monthly, quarterly, annually, or custom); the date of the last interaction (manually logged or auto-detected from a connected messaging/email channel); an automatically calculated next reconnect date (last interaction + frequency interval); and an optional scheduled future interaction (a specific date when contact is already planned). The People list highlights people whose next reconnect date has passed or is approaching. A 'Reconnect due' filtered view shows only these people, sorted by most overdue first. This prevents important relationships from silently lapsing without the user noticing."},
      {label:"Custom data fields — flexible person profiles",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Different relationships require tracking different information. A supplier contact needs a company name, VAT number, and product categories. A close friend needs a birthday, spouse's name, and shared interests. Plenix does not impose a fixed schema. Each person's profile supports user-defined fields of multiple types: Email (with click-to-compose and direct message action); Phone (with click-to-call and WhatsApp message action); Link (general URL or platform-specific profile link — LinkedIn, Instagram, Twitter/X, GitHub, personal site); Social profiles (recognized platforms get branded icons); Date — one-time (e.g. a meeting date, anniversary); Date — recurring (e.g. birthday, work anniversary — automatically recalculates each year); Birthday (special case of recurring date — auto-calculates age and years known); and Text/notes (formatted, multi-paragraph, supports markdown). Field names are fully customizable — the user labels each field (e.g. 'Dog's name', 'Preferred coffee', 'Last gift given'). There is no required field except a name."},
      {label:"Event and date tracking — birthdays, anniversaries, upcoming events",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Missing a birthday or anniversary damages a relationship. Plenix tracks all date-type fields across all contacts and surfaces upcoming events proactively. The Events view shows a chronological list of upcoming dates across the entire contact database: birthdays, anniversaries, scheduled reconnects, and any custom date fields. Each event shows the person's name, the event type, the date, and how many days away it is. For birthday fields, the person's age is automatically calculated and shown. Optional advance notifications fire a configurable number of days before the event (e.g. 7 days before a birthday). The Calendar section also shows these events when the relevant calendar is enabled, so relationship events appear alongside work and personal calendar items."},
      {label:"Interaction history and memory timeline",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to remember what they talked about last time, what they gave as a gift, what the person mentioned about their family, or what shared experience they had. Each person in Plenix has a chronological interaction timeline. Entries in the timeline can include: logged interactions (a note with date, channel, and a summary of what was discussed); shared events or experiences (a memory the user wants to preserve); linked tasks (a follow-up action that arose from an interaction, linked bidirectionally); and linked journal entries (if the user journals, entries can be associated with one or more people so the full context is accessible from the person's profile). The timeline provides continuity — the user can scroll back through months or years of interactions and quickly recall the full context of a relationship before reaching out. Entries can be added manually or captured automatically from connected messaging/email channels."},
      {label:"Notes and context — running record per person",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Every conversation reveals things worth remembering — someone mentioned they are looking for a new job, they have a child starting school, they are allergic to shellfish, they prefer to be contacted by WhatsApp not email. The Notes field on each person's profile is a formatted, persistent text area that the user maintains over time. It supports markdown formatting (bold, bullets, headings) and can be as long as needed. Unlike interaction timeline entries (which are date-stamped events), notes are a living document — a running record of context that the user edits and updates. The notes field is searchable across all contacts, so typing 'shellfish' finds the person who mentioned the allergy even if the user doesn't remember the name."},
      {label:"Task integration — follow-up actions from contacts",status:"planned",desc:"From any person's profile, the user can create a task linked to that contact. The task appears in the Tasks section with the contact's name as context, and in the contact's profile under a 'Linked tasks' tab. Examples: 'Send proposal to [name]', 'Follow up with [name] about the contract', 'Buy birthday gift for [name]'. Tasks created this way inherit the contact's project association if one exists. Completing the task in the Tasks section marks it as resolved in the contact's profile. This bidirectional link means the user never needs to duplicate information — the task lives in one place but is visible from both the task list and the contact record."},
      {label:"Privacy and security — encrypted contact data",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Contact profiles contain sensitive personal information — family details, private notes, health information, financial context. This data requires protection beyond normal app security. Plenix applies encryption to stored contact data and interaction histories. An optional additional access control (device PIN or biometric) can be required to open the People section, separate from the main app login. This is particularly important if the user's device is shared or if the contact database contains sensitive relationship notes that should not be visible to casual observers. Encrypted export is available so the user can back up their contact data to a file without it being readable if the backup is accessed by a third party."},
      {label:"People section sidebar — tiers, views, and filters",status:"planned",desc:"The People sidebar reflects the full hierarchy and filter options: All Contacts (total count); Needs Attention (contacts flagged for relationship maintenance); Reconnect Due (contacts whose reconnect interval has elapsed); Upcoming Events (birthdays and anniversaries in the next 30 days); then the relationship tier sections — Family, Extended Family, Close Connections, Casual Connections, Acquaintances, Professional, Distant. Each tier section shows a count. Below tiers, Groups (Clients, Contractors, etc.) appear as before. A search bar at the top of the sidebar filters the visible list by name across all tiers. The user can create custom groups (e.g. 'Book club', 'Surf friends', 'Investors') as additional organizational layers that cut across the tier hierarchy."},
      {label:"Widget action icons — message and call",status:"done",desc:"In the dashboard widget, each contact row has two circular action buttons: a blue message bubble (navigates to that contact's thread in the Messaging section) and a green phone circle (initiates a call). Both use event stopPropagation to prevent row selection on click. Both have hover state color changes."}
    ]},
    {area:"Procurement & Sourcing",color:"brown",items:[
      {label:"Supplier discovery — AI-powered global search",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user needs to find qualified manufacturers or suppliers for a product, component, or category — globally — without spending days manually searching directories, trade show records, and industry databases. Plenix's supplier discovery feature uses AI to search across a database of millions of manufacturers spanning 200+ countries, sourced from customs records, trade show exhibitor lists, B2B directories (Alibaba, Global Sources, Kompass), and verified business registries. The user enters a product description, category, material, or specification and the AI returns a ranked list of recommended suppliers with profiles showing: company name, country, primary product categories, certifications held (ISO, CE, RoHS, etc.), estimated annual revenue, number of employees, and a match score explaining why the AI recommended them. Results can be filtered by country, MOQ range, lead time, certification, and diversity status. This is the starting point of every sourcing workflow in Plenix."},
      {label:"Supplier profiles — verified data and hierarchy mapping",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Before engaging a supplier, the user needs to know they are dealing with the correct legal entity, understand the corporate structure, and trust that the contact information and credentials are accurate. Plenix resolves each supplier to its verified legal entity — distinguishing between trading companies, manufacturers, subsidiaries, and parent corporations. Corporate hierarchy mapping shows the full family tree: parent company, subsidiaries, related entities, and known trade names. Each profile is enriched with 30+ attributes including: registered business name, tax ID / VAT number, incorporation country, industry classification (NAICS/HS codes), diversity certifications (woman-owned, minority-owned, small business), active certifications (ISO 9001, ISO 14001, IATF 16949, etc.), and primary export markets. This eliminates the 'vendor master data tax' — the hidden cost of sourcing decisions made on incomplete, duplicate, or mismatched supplier records."},
      {label:"Supplier outreach — automated RFQs and follow-ups",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Once the user has identified candidate suppliers, they need to send Request for Quotation (RFQ) emails, track responses, and follow up with non-responders — a process that is time-consuming and repetitive at scale. Plenix automates the RFQ workflow: the user defines the product specification, quantity tiers, required lead times, packaging requirements, and target price. Plenix generates a professional RFQ document and sends it to selected suppliers via email or the supplier's preferred channel. Follow-up messages are sent automatically on a configured schedule (e.g. day 3, day 7, day 14) if no response is received. All supplier replies are captured, parsed, and displayed in a unified comparison view so the user can evaluate quotes side by side without managing a complex email thread. The user makes the decisions — Plenix handles the coordination."},
      {label:"RFQ comparison and supplier shortlisting",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: After receiving quotes from multiple suppliers, the user needs to compare them objectively — not just on price but on lead time, payment terms, MOQ, sample cost, certifications, and quality indicators. The RFQ comparison table shows all responding suppliers as rows with quote attributes as columns. The user can weight each attribute (e.g. price 40%, lead time 30%, certifications 30%) and Plenix calculates a weighted score for each supplier. Columns can be sorted and filtered. A 'Shortlist' action flags the top candidates for the next stage (sampling or negotiation). Non-selected suppliers receive a polite decline message automatically if the user chooses. The shortlist is saved to the relevant Project in Plenix so it is accessible from the project's Files and Notes."},
      {label:"Product ideation — from inspiration to BOM",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user has a product idea, a reference image, or a competitor product they want to develop or improve. Plenix's product ideation tools let the user upload an image or describe a product concept, and the AI generates: product variations (different materials, colors, configurations), a first-pass Bill of Materials (BOM) listing likely components, materials, and sub-assemblies, and suggested product categories to search for relevant suppliers. The BOM is editable — the user can add, remove, or modify line items — and each BOM line can be directly connected to a supplier discovery search, so clicking a component immediately shows candidate suppliers for that specific part. This closes the loop between concept and sourcing in a single workflow."},
      {label:"Vendor master management — cleanse, deduplicate, enrich",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Users who manage procurement at scale accumulate a vendor master file (a database of all known suppliers) that becomes cluttered with duplicates, outdated records, missing attributes, and mismatched legal names over time. This 'supplier data tax' slows down every downstream decision. Plenix's vendor master tools let the user import an existing supplier list (CSV, Excel, or sync from an ERP), run an AI deduplication pass that identifies records referring to the same legal entity, resolve conflicts (which record is the authoritative one), and enrich all records with verified attributes. Ongoing enrichment keeps records fresh — when a supplier's certification expires, their contact details change, or a corporate acquisition occurs, Plenix flags the affected records for review. The cleaned vendor master can be exported back to source systems (SAP Ariba, Coupa, Oracle) or used natively within Plenix."},
      {label:"Supplier relationship tracking",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user works with multiple suppliers across multiple projects and needs to track the full history of each relationship — communications, orders, quotes, samples, issues, and notes — in one place rather than scattered across email and spreadsheets. Each supplier in Plenix has a relationship record that aggregates: all RFQs sent and received, all email threads (via connected email), all notes added by the user, any tasks or follow-ups linked to that supplier, sample and order history, and a relationship health indicator based on response rate, on-time delivery, and quality issues logged. This is the procurement equivalent of a CRM — Plenix acts as the system of record for every supplier relationship, linked to the project and contact records that already exist in the app."}
    ]},
    {area:"Projects",color:"purple",items:[
      {label:"Color-coded project cards",status:"done",desc:"Each project has an assigned color used in three places: a 3px solid left border on the card, the project name text tinted in that color, and a subtle background wash (color at ~7% opacity) on hover. This creates immediate visual recognition when scanning a list of projects without being visually heavy."},
      {label:"Project detail hero header",status:"done",desc:"When you open a project, the detail view opens with a hero header: a 4px colored accent border on the left edge, the project name in the project color at 22px, a category label above the name, and a description below. Add File and Share buttons on the right. A live progress bar fills proportionally based on task completion, alongside file count, note count, and sync status."},
      {label:"Project detail tabs",status:"done",desc:"Four tabs: Overview, Files, Notes, and Tasks. The active tab's underline uses the project's own color. Files and Tasks tabs show live count badges. Switching tabs replaces the panel content without navigation."},
      {label:"Tasks tab with live completion",status:"done",desc:"Progress bar at the top (color-matched to the project) and a task list below. Each task has a live checkbox — checking turns it green, fades the row to 60% opacity, applies strikethrough. Progress bar and percentage re-calculate instantly."},
      {label:"Project structure — tasks, stages, roadmaps, sections",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A project is more than a list of tasks — it has workflow stages, a timeline of phases, and sections that group related work. Each project in Plenix contains: Tasks (individual work items), Workflow stages (columns defining where each task sits in the process), Roadmaps (one or more Gantt-style timelines showing work across dates), and Sections or phases (groupings within the board or roadmap — e.g. Phase 1: Discovery, Phase 2: Build). Projects are fully independent — one project's structure does not affect any other. Multiple projects can be active simultaneously, each with their own board, columns, tasks, and roadmap."},
      {label:"Kanban board view — drag-and-drop workflow stages",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to see all tasks organized by workflow stage. The Kanban board shows tasks as cards in vertical columns. Each column represents a workflow stage — fully customizable per project (e.g. Todo / In Progress / Review / Done, or Backlog / Scoping / Design / Dev / QA / Shipped). Task cards show: title, priority dot, due date, assignee avatar, and tags. Dragging a card between columns changes its status. Dragging within a column reorders tasks. A count badge on each column header shows how many tasks it contains. An 'Add task' button at the bottom of each column creates a new task directly in that stage. No fixed structure is imposed."},
      {label:"Column customization — per-project workflow stages",status:"planned",desc:"Each project defines its own columns independently. Columns are created, renamed, reordered by dragging, and deleted from the board view. One column is designated as Done — tasks moved here count toward the completion percentage. A column can be archived (hidden but tasks preserved). Optional WIP limit (Work In Progress cap) per column — exceeding it highlights the column in amber. Column colors are optional."},
      {label:"List view — structured task list within a project",status:"planned",desc:"An alternative to the Kanban board. All project tasks in a flat or grouped list: checkbox, priority, title, status (which column it is in), due date, tags, assignee. Groupable by: column/stage, priority, due date, or section/phase. Sorting is independent of Kanban column order. Inline editing on all fields directly in the row. Bulk actions: select multiple tasks and update stage, priority, due date, or tags simultaneously. Fastest view for triaging many tasks at once."},
      {label:"Roadmap view — Gantt-style timeline planning",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user needs to plan a project over time — seeing which phases overlap, when milestones land, and whether the timeline is realistic. The Roadmap view is a Gantt-style horizontal timeline. Each task or section appears as a horizontal bar spanning its start and end dates. Bars are draggable to move dates; drag either end to resize. Three zoom levels: Month (entire project arc), Week (current and adjacent weeks), Day (hour-by-hour for the next few days). Status colors: planned (gray), in progress (blue), completed (green), blocked (red). Dependencies between tasks shown as connecting arrows. Milestone markers (diamonds) can be placed on any date for key deliverables."},
      {label:"Multiple roadmaps per project",status:"planned",desc:"A single project can have more than one roadmap — e.g. a Development roadmap and a Marketing roadmap running in parallel. Each roadmap is a named tab within the Roadmap view. Roadmaps share the same task pool — the same task can appear on multiple roadmaps with different date framing. Roadmaps can also contain phase-level items representing broader milestones rather than individual tasks."},
      {label:"Project colors and visual identity",status:"planned",desc:"Every project is assigned a color used consistently throughout Plenix: the 3px left border on cards, tinted name text, active tab underline, progress bar color, Calendar timeline block color, and project badge on linked tasks, notes, and bookmarks. Color is the primary visual project identifier system-wide. 16 palette colors available at creation; custom hex input also supported."},
      {label:"Progress tracking — completion rates and task counts",status:"planned",desc:"Progress tracked two ways: task completion count (e.g. 12 of 30 tasks done) and completion rate percentage (40%). Visible in the hero header, project card, and dashboard Projects widget. Calculated from tasks in the designated Done column. A separate manual project status is set by the user: Active, Paused, Completed, or Archived — independent of the calculated percentage."},
      {label:"Project archiving and restoration",status:"planned",desc:"Completed or inactive projects can be archived — removed from all active views (list, dashboard widget, sidebar, task filters) without deleting any data. Accessible from an Archived section in the Projects sidebar. All tasks, notes, files, roadmaps, and history are fully preserved. Restoration is one click and reinstates the project in all views exactly as it was. Settings → Projects has toggles to include or exclude archived and paused projects from specific views."},
      {label:"Project sidebar — grouped navigation with task counts",status:"done",desc:"The Projects sidebar groups projects by tag/area (Personal, Client Work, Research, Travel, Finance). Each project listed with color dot, name, status indicator, and task count badge. Clicking navigates to project detail. Supports the same collapse/resize behavior as all other Plenix sidebars."},
      {label:"Sub-projects — parent/child project hierarchy",status:"planned",desc:"A project can contain child projects (sub-projects), enabling client account structures where a top-level entity (e.g. Landmark Office Partners) owns multiple discrete projects (Homepage Redesign, Product Launch, Brand Guidelines). The parent project acts as a hub — its dashboard aggregates status, task completion, and overdue items from all children in one view. The sidebar shows the parent with an expand arrow revealing child projects as an indented sub-tree. Each child is fully independent — its own board, tasks, roadmap, files, contacts, and mood boards — but rolls up into the parent reporting. Nesting is one level deep by default. A child project can be promoted to standalone at any time."},
      {label:"Project dashboard tab — customizable drag-and-drop widget canvas",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Dashboard tab — a drag-and-drop customizable widget canvas scoped to that project. Widgets: Upcoming tasks, Overdue tasks, Client action required (tasks tagged awaiting external input), Recently completed, Phase progress bar, Budget gauge, Roadmap summary (next 3 milestones), Team members with role badges, Goals/KPI cards (user-defined metric cards with label, current value, and target — e.g. 55% retention, 50K DAUs), Quick links (project saved links as clickable chips), and Activity feed (last 10 events). Widgets are added from a picker, positioned by drag-and-drop, and sized by dragging corners. Layout persists per project. Sub-project rollup widget shows aggregated status across all child projects."},
      {label:"Project contacts — people linked with roles",status:"planned",desc:"Any contact from the main Contacts section can be associated with a project with a role: Owner (internal, full control), Team Member (internal, assigned tasks and files), Client (external, portal access), Consultant (external, hired or manually added), Reviewer (approves deliverables), Stakeholder (read-only). The People tab lists all linked contacts grouped by role showing avatar, name, company, and contact icons. Clicking opens their full Contacts profile. Multiple contacts per role allowed. Removing a contact from a project does not delete them from Contacts — it removes only the association."},
      {label:"Project links — named quick links with type and auto-detected icon",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Links tab for saving relevant URLs: Figma files, Jira boards, Miro boards, Google Docs, reference articles, client websites, supplier pages. Each link has: URL, display name, type (Design, Dev, Client, Reference, Supplier, Tool, Other), and an icon auto-detected from the domain (Figma, Notion, Google, GitHub, Miro, Jira, Confluence, Slack and hundreds of others get branded icons; unknown domains get a globe). Links display as clickable cards with icon, name, type badge, and domain. Links saved in a project are automatically tagged to that project in the master Bookmarks library — the association is bidirectional. Any master bookmark can also be associated with a project to surface it in the project Links tab."},
      {label:"Master bookmarks — Raindrop.io-style universal link library",status:"planned",desc:"Plenix has a master Bookmarks section inspired by Raindrop.io. Every link saved anywhere in Plenix — from a project Links tab, the browser bookmarklet, the browser extension, or manually — lands in this central library. Each bookmark has: URL, title, description, favicon, screenshot thumbnail (captured at save time), tags, collection/folder, project associations (zero or many), date saved, and source. Collections are hierarchical folders. Smart collections auto-populate by rules (e.g. all bookmarks tagged supplier or all from figma.com). Filtering by project shows only bookmarks for that project. A bookmark can be associated with multiple projects. Full-text search includes captured article body text. Raindrop.io import: existing Raindrop libraries importable via their API or an exported HTML file."},
      {label:"Project files — OneDrive via Microsoft Graph API",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Files tab connected to OneDrive via the Microsoft Graph API (graph.microsoft.com) — the unified API covering OneDrive Personal, OneDrive for Business, and SharePoint. Connection established once via OAuth in Settings, Connectors, OneDrive. Within the Files tab the user browses their OneDrive folder tree and links specific files or folders to the project. Linked files show filename, type icon, last modified date, and modified-by user. Clicking opens the file in the appropriate Microsoft 365 web viewer (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, PDF) within a Plenix panel. Microsoft Graph change notification webhooks notify Plenix when a linked file is modified, updating the indicator in real time without polling. Files are not copied into Plenix — they are referenced by their Graph item ID. Uploading from within Plenix posts the file to a configured project folder in OneDrive via the Graph upload API."},
      {label:"Project images — gallery with upload, URL, and Unsplash sources",status:"planned",desc:"The Images tab is a media gallery holding all images for the project. Images are added from three sources: Upload (drag-and-drop or file picker, JPEG/PNG/WebP/GIF/SVG stored in Plenix file storage), URL (paste an image URL, Plenix fetches and stores a copy), and Unsplash (built-in search panel using the Unsplash API, full-resolution download stored with attribution). Each image has a title, description, tags, and date added. The gallery renders in masonry grid by default with a toggle to uniform grid. Clicking an image opens a lightbox with metadata. Images in the gallery are the source pool for mood boards."},
      {label:"Mood boards — named visual collections per project",status:"planned",desc:"A project can have multiple named mood boards. Each mood board is a curated visual collection sourced from: images in the project gallery, images from any URL pasted directly, Unsplash searches, and uploaded images (also added to the gallery). Two layout modes: Grid (uniform tile size, configurable columns) and Masonry (Pinterest-style varied heights). Images reorder by drag-and-drop and can be captioned. Each mood board has a name, optional description, and creation date. Multiple mood boards per project separate visual directions (e.g. Mood Board A: minimalist, Mood Board B: bold). Mood boards are shareable as a read-only public link for presenting directions to a client without full project access."},
      {label:"Project identity — status, tags, icon, and client association",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a full identity layer: Status (Active, At Risk, Off Track, Paused, Completed, Archived — shown as a colored badge on the card and hero header); Tags (freeform, multi-value, color-coded — Long-term, VIP, Real Estate, Rush, Internal — used for filtering and grouping); Icon or emoji (chosen at creation, displayed in sidebar and header); and Client association (linking a project to a Client-type contact surfaces the project in that contact profile under Projects and enables the client portal for that contact). Status can be set manually or updated by automations (e.g. auto-mark At Risk if overdue task count exceeds a threshold)."},
      {label:"Activity feed — real-time project event log",status:"planned",desc:"Every project has a persistent activity feed: task created, completed, or reassigned; file uploaded or modified; comment posted; status changed; contact added; link saved; mood board created; sub-project added; phase advanced; change request submitted or approved; payment request raised. Each event shows: actor avatar and name, what they did, which item was affected (linked), and a timestamp. The feed is searchable and filterable by event type. Team members can post manual status updates that appear as announcements distinct from automated entries. The activity feed is the single source of truth for what happened on a project and when."},
      {label:"Sub-projects — parent/child project hierarchy",status:"planned",desc:"A project can contain child projects enabling client account structures where a top-level entity owns multiple discrete projects. The parent project dashboard aggregates status task completion and overdue items from all children. The sidebar shows the parent with an expand arrow revealing child projects as an indented sub-tree. Each child is fully independent with its own board tasks roadmap files contacts and mood boards but rolls up into the parent reporting. Nesting is one level deep by default. A child project can be promoted to standalone at any time."},
      {label:"Project dashboard tab — customizable drag-and-drop widget canvas",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Dashboard tab that is a drag-and-drop customizable widget canvas scoped to that project. Available widgets: Upcoming tasks, Overdue tasks, Client action required (tasks awaiting external input), Recently completed, Phase progress bar, Budget gauge, Roadmap summary (next 3 milestones), Team members with role badges, Goals/KPI cards (user-defined metric cards with label current value and target), Quick links (project saved links as clickable chips), and Activity feed. Widgets are added from a picker positioned by drag-and-drop and sized by dragging corners. Layout persists per project. A sub-project rollup widget aggregates status across all child projects."},
      {label:"Project contacts — people linked with roles",status:"planned",desc:"Any contact from the main Contacts section can be associated with a project with a role: Owner (internal full control), Team Member (internal assigned tasks and files), Client (external portal access), Consultant (external hired or manually added), Reviewer (approves deliverables), Stakeholder (read-only). The People tab lists all linked contacts grouped by role. Clicking opens their full Contacts profile. Multiple contacts per role are allowed. Removing a contact from a project removes only the association not the contact record."},
      {label:"Project links — named quick links with type and auto-detected icon",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Links tab for saving relevant URLs including Figma files Jira boards Miro boards Google Docs reference articles and supplier pages. Each link has a URL display name type (Design, Dev, Client, Reference, Supplier, Tool, Other) and an icon auto-detected from the domain. Hundreds of domains including Figma Notion Google GitHub Miro Jira Confluence and Slack get branded icons. Links saved in a project are automatically tagged to that project in the master Bookmarks library. Any master bookmark can also be associated with a project to surface it in the project Links tab. The association is bidirectional."},
      {label:"Master bookmarks — Raindrop.io-style universal link library",status:"planned",desc:"Plenix has a master Bookmarks section inspired by Raindrop.io. Every link saved anywhere in Plenix lands in this central library. Each bookmark has a URL title description favicon screenshot thumbnail (captured at save time) tags collection/folder project associations (zero or many) date saved and source. Collections are hierarchical folders. Smart collections auto-populate by rules such as all bookmarks tagged supplier or all from figma.com. A bookmark can be associated with multiple projects. Full-text search includes captured article body text. Existing Raindrop.io libraries can be imported via their API or an exported HTML file."},
      {label:"Project files — OneDrive via Microsoft Graph API",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Files tab connected to OneDrive via the Microsoft Graph API at graph.microsoft.com which covers OneDrive Personal OneDrive for Business and SharePoint. Connection is established once via OAuth in Settings Connectors OneDrive. The user browses their OneDrive folder tree and links specific files or folders to the project. Linked files show filename type icon last modified date and modified-by user. Clicking opens the file in the appropriate Microsoft 365 web viewer within a Plenix panel. Microsoft Graph change notification webhooks notify Plenix when a linked file is modified updating the indicator in real time. Files are not copied into Plenix but referenced by their Graph item ID. Uploading from within Plenix posts the file to a configured project folder in OneDrive via the Graph upload API."},
      {label:"Project images — gallery with upload URL and Unsplash sources",status:"planned",desc:"The Images tab is a media gallery holding all images for the project. Images are added from three sources: Upload (drag-and-drop or file picker supporting JPEG PNG WebP GIF and SVG stored in Plenix file storage), URL (paste any image URL and Plenix fetches and stores a copy), and Unsplash (built-in search panel using the Unsplash API with full-resolution download stored with attribution). Each image has a title description tags and date added. The gallery renders in masonry grid by default with a toggle to uniform grid. Clicking an image opens a lightbox. Images in the gallery are the source pool for mood boards."},
      {label:"Mood boards — named visual collections per project",status:"planned",desc:"A project can have multiple named mood boards. Each is a curated visual collection sourced from images in the project gallery, images from any pasted URL, Unsplash searches, and uploaded images. Two layout modes: Grid (uniform tile size configurable columns) and Masonry (Pinterest-style varied heights). Images reorder by drag-and-drop and can have captions. Each mood board has a name optional description and creation date. Multiple mood boards per project separate visual directions such as Mood Board A minimalist and Mood Board B bold. Mood boards are shareable as a read-only public link for presenting visual directions to a client without full project access."},
      {label:"Project identity — status tags icon and client association",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a full identity layer. Status: Active At Risk Off Track Paused Completed Archived shown as a colored badge on the card and hero header. Tags: freeform multi-value color-coded labels like Long-term VIP Real Estate Rush or Internal used for filtering and grouping. Icon or emoji: chosen at creation displayed in the sidebar and header. Client association: linking a project to a Client-type contact surfaces the project in that contact profile and enables the client portal for that contact. Status can be set manually or updated by automations such as auto-marking At Risk when the overdue task count exceeds a threshold."},
      {label:"Activity feed — real-time project event log",status:"planned",desc:"Every project has a persistent activity feed logging every significant event: task created completed or reassigned; file uploaded or modified; comment posted; status changed; contact added; link saved; mood board created; sub-project added; phase advanced; change request submitted or approved; payment request raised. Each event shows the actor avatar and name what they did which item was affected and a timestamp. The feed is searchable and filterable by event type. Team members can post manual status updates that appear as announcements distinct from automated entries. The activity feed is the single source of truth for what happened on a project and when."},
      {label:"Sub-projects — parent/child project hierarchy",status:"planned",desc:"A project can contain child projects enabling client account structures where a top-level entity owns multiple discrete projects. The parent project dashboard aggregates status task completion and overdue items from all children. The sidebar shows the parent with an expand arrow revealing child projects as an indented sub-tree. Each child is fully independent with its own board tasks roadmap files contacts and mood boards but rolls up into parent reporting. Nesting is one level deep by default. A child can be promoted to standalone at any time."},
      {label:"Project dashboard tab — customizable drag-and-drop widget canvas",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Dashboard tab with a drag-and-drop customizable widget canvas scoped to that project. Available widgets include Upcoming tasks, Overdue tasks, Client action required (tasks awaiting external input), Recently completed, Phase progress bar, Budget gauge, Roadmap summary showing next 3 milestones, Team members with role badges, Goals and KPI cards with user-defined label current value and target, Quick links as clickable chips, and Activity feed. Widgets are added from a picker positioned by drag-and-drop and sized by dragging corners. Layout persists per project. A sub-project rollup widget aggregates status across all child projects."},
      {label:"Project contacts — people linked with roles",status:"planned",desc:"Any contact from the main Contacts section can be associated with a project with a defined role: Owner (internal full control), Team Member (internal assigned tasks and files), Client (external portal access), Consultant (external hired or manually added), Reviewer (approves deliverables), or Stakeholder (read-only). The People tab lists all linked contacts grouped by role. Clicking any contact opens their full profile. Multiple contacts per role are allowed. Removing a contact from a project removes only the association and does not delete the contact record."},
      {label:"Project links — named quick links with type and auto-detected icon",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Links tab for saving relevant URLs including Figma files, Jira boards, Miro boards, Google Docs, reference articles, and supplier pages. Each link has a URL, display name, type (Design, Dev, Client, Reference, Supplier, Tool, or Other), and an icon auto-detected from the domain. Hundreds of domains including Figma, Notion, Google, GitHub, Miro, Jira, Confluence, and Slack get branded icons. Links saved in a project are automatically tagged to that project in the master Bookmarks library creating a bidirectional association. Any master bookmark can also be manually associated with a project to surface it in that project Links tab."},
      {label:"Master bookmarks — Raindrop.io-style universal link library",status:"planned",desc:"Plenix has a master Bookmarks section inspired by Raindrop.io. Every link saved anywhere in Plenix lands in this central library. Each bookmark has a URL, title, description, favicon, screenshot thumbnail captured at save time, tags, collection folder, project associations (zero or many), date saved, and source. Collections are hierarchical folders. Smart collections auto-populate by rules such as all bookmarks tagged supplier or all from figma.com. A bookmark can be associated with multiple projects. Full-text search includes captured article body text. Existing Raindrop.io libraries can be imported via their API or an exported HTML file."},
      {label:"Project files — OneDrive via Microsoft Graph API",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a Files tab connected to OneDrive via the Microsoft Graph API at graph.microsoft.com which covers OneDrive Personal, OneDrive for Business, and SharePoint. Connection is established once via OAuth in Settings Connectors OneDrive. The user browses their OneDrive folder tree and links specific files or folders to the project. Linked files show filename, type icon, last modified date, and modified-by user. Clicking opens the file in the appropriate Microsoft 365 web viewer within a Plenix panel. Microsoft Graph change notification webhooks notify Plenix when a linked file is modified. Files are referenced by their Graph item ID and not copied into Plenix. Uploading from within Plenix posts the file to a configured project folder via the Graph upload API."},
      {label:"Project images — gallery with upload URL and Unsplash sources",status:"planned",desc:"The Images tab is a media gallery holding all images for the project. Images are added from three sources: Upload supporting JPEG PNG WebP GIF and SVG stored in Plenix file storage; URL where Plenix fetches and stores a copy of any pasted image URL; and Unsplash where a built-in search panel using the Unsplash API downloads full-resolution images with attribution. Each image has a title, description, tags, and date added. The gallery renders in masonry grid by default with a toggle to uniform grid. Clicking an image opens a lightbox. Images in the gallery are the source pool for mood boards."},
      {label:"Mood boards — named visual collections per project",status:"planned",desc:"A project can have multiple named mood boards. Each is a curated visual collection sourced from images in the project gallery, images from any pasted URL, Unsplash searches, and direct uploads. Two layout modes are available: Grid with uniform tile size and configurable columns, and Masonry with Pinterest-style varied heights. Images reorder by drag-and-drop and can have captions. Each mood board has a name, optional description, and creation date. Multiple mood boards separate visual directions such as a minimalist direction and a bold colorful direction. Mood boards are shareable as a read-only public link for presenting visual directions to a client without full project access."},
      {label:"Project identity — status tags icon and client association",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has a full identity layer. Status options are Active, At Risk, Off Track, Paused, Completed, and Archived shown as a colored badge on the card and hero header. Tags are freeform multi-value color-coded labels such as Long-term, VIP, Real Estate, Rush, or Internal used for filtering and grouping. An icon or emoji can be chosen at creation and displayed in the sidebar and header. Client association links a project to a Client-type contact surfacing the project in that contact profile and enabling the client portal for that contact. Status can be set manually or updated by automations such as auto-marking At Risk when the overdue task count exceeds a threshold."},
      {label:"Activity feed — real-time project event log",status:"planned",desc:"Every project has a persistent activity feed logging every significant event including task created completed or reassigned, file uploaded or modified, comment posted, status changed, contact added, link saved, mood board created, sub-project added, phase advanced, change request submitted or approved, and payment request raised. Each event shows the actor avatar and name, what they did, the affected item linked for navigation, and a timestamp. The feed is searchable and filterable by event type. Team members can post manual status updates that appear as announcements distinct from automated entries. The activity feed is the single source of truth for project history."}
    ]},
    {area:"RFP & Consultant Platform",color:"brown",items:[
      {label:"Project / RFP creation — structured proposal document",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A project owner needs to create a structured project specification that can be shared with consultants who will bid on the work. This is a formal RFP/RFQ document that defines the engagement clearly enough for a consultant to price it accurately. The creation form collects: Basic info (product name, authors); Content sections in rich text (About Us, Evaluation criteria, Deliverables, Payment terms, NDA with e-signature requirement, Release criteria); Purpose section (target audience, objective, product description); Timeline (deadline, milestones, dependencies); and Media attachments — images are watermarked and low-quality during bidding, unlocked to full resolution after a consultant is hired, and documents require an annotation/description field. The structure is section-based with rich text editors per section, giving the RFP professional document quality rather than a form-fill feel."},
      {label:"Consultant discovery — expertise matching and invite links",status:"planned",desc:"Consultants find projects two ways: direct invite (the project owner generates a link and sends it) or expertise matching (the project is visible to any consultant whose expertise tags overlap with the project's category). Visibility settings: Public (any matched consultant can see and bid) or Private (invite-only). Consultants can request access to private projects; the owner reviews and approves. Invite links expire after a configurable period. Before viewing full project details including sensitive documents, consultants must accept the project's NDA — enforced as a platform gate before access is granted."},
      {label:"Proposal submission — bid structure and work samples",status:"planned",desc:"Each consultant submits: Total offer (USD or configured currency), Milestone-based cost breakdown (how the total splits across deliverables — e.g. 30% on design approval, 40% on delivery, 30% on final sign-off), Proposal narrative (approach, methodology, why they are the right fit), Work sample uploads (documents and images), and External portfolio links. The platform detects and renders icons for known platforms: Alibaba, ArtStation, Behance, Dribbble, Fiverr, GitHub, Instagram, LinkedIn, Upwork, personal portfolio, Pinterest, VK, and others. Submitted proposals queue in the project owner's Offers Table. Consultants can save drafts before submitting."},
      {label:"Offers table — compare, rate, filter, and select bids",status:"planned",desc:"Each project has an Offers Table showing all submitted proposals. Columns: Company, Contact person (with communication icons for WhatsApp, email, phone, LinkedIn), Status (Draft / Submitted / Under Review / Shortlisted / Counter-offered / Accepted / Rejected), Decline reason (hidden unless Rejected), Star rating (1–5 set by owner), Internal notes, Counter-offer amount (hidden unless active), Portfolio icons, Contact icons. Table features: sortable, filterable by status/rating, groupable by expertise/country, and a side-by-side comparison mode (select 2–4 proposals, all fields aligned in columns). This is the primary evaluation surface — the owner works through submissions here rather than opening each proposal individually."},
      {label:"Proposal lifecycle and counter-offer negotiation",status:"planned",desc:"Proposals move through: Draft → Submitted → Under Review → Shortlisted → Counter-offered → Accepted / Rejected. Counter-offers are handled in-platform: the owner enters a counter amount and note; the consultant receives a notification and can accept, decline, or counter back. Each iteration is recorded with timestamps for a clear negotiation trail. Per-proposal messaging allows direct communication scoped to a specific bid — visible only to the two parties. Once Accepted, the status changes to Hired and the project moves into execution. Rejected proposals show a decline reason visible to the consultant."},
      {label:"Project lifecycle — 6 fixed phases from Startup to Closure",status:"planned",desc:"Once a consultant is hired, the project enters a 6-phase lifecycle: Startup (kick-off, access setup, NDA counter-signing), Initiation (requirements finalization, stakeholder alignment, project charter), Planning (task scheduling, risk identification, resource confirmation), Design (creative or technical work, review cycles, approval gates), Execution (primary delivery, progress tracking, issue management, change requests), and Closure (final delivery, acceptance sign-off, invoice settlement, lessons learned, archive). Each phase has a status (Not started / In progress / Complete) shown as a visual progress bar on the project dashboard. Tasks belong to exactly one phase. Phase names are customizable per project; the 6-phase structure reflects industry-standard methodology."},
      {label:"Smart task system — phase-based with auto-generated recommendations",status:"planned",desc:"When a project enters a new phase, the system recommends a standard task set for that phase based on project type. For a branding project entering Design phase: Create mood board, Develop logo concepts, Present initial concepts, Incorporate feedback round 1, Finalize for approval. The owner accepts, modifies, or declines each suggestion. Task features: Dependencies (task B blocks until task A is complete, with critical path highlighting), Subtasks, Recurring tasks, Priority levels (Urgent/High/Medium/Low), Time tracking (hours logged), Milestone-based grouping, Deadline tracking with overdue highlights. Tasks assigned to a consultant appear in their Consultant Portal dashboard."},
      {label:"Risk, issue, assumption, and constraint log — RIAC",status:"planned",desc:"Beyond tasks, projects track four item types: Risks (potential future problems — likelihood, impact, owner, mitigation plan), Issues (current problems — severity, owner, resolution plan), Assumptions (things the project depends on being true — if disproved, triggers a change request), and Constraints (fixed limitations — budget ceiling, regulatory requirement, technology lock-in). The RIAC log is a table on the project dashboard. Risks can be converted to Issues with one click when they materialize. All items have owners assigned from the project team. The log is included in auto-generated status reports."},
      {label:"Change management — formal change request approval workflow",status:"planned",desc:"Scope changes follow a structured change request (CR) flow. A CR is created by either party and contains: description of proposed change, reason (client request / error correction / scope clarification / external dependency), timeline impact (days added or removed), cost impact (additional or reduced amount), and supporting documents. Flow: Drafted → Submitted → Under Review → Approved / Rejected. Approved CRs automatically update the project timeline and trigger a revised payment adjustment. All CRs are logged chronologically — the project's change history is a permanent record of every scope modification and its approval status."},
      {label:"Financial system — milestone payments, invoices, multi-currency",status:"planned",desc:"Payments are milestone-based: when a milestone is accepted by the project owner, the associated payment is triggered. The consultant raises a Payment Request linked to the milestone; the owner approves, initiating payment. Invoice management: consultants generate invoices within the platform pre-populated with project name, milestone description, amount, payment terms, and both parties' details. Payment history shows all transactions per project with status (Pending / Processing / Paid). Gateway integration: Stripe, PayPal, bank transfer reference tracking. Multi-currency: USD, EUR, GBP, and other configured currencies with exchange rate display. Planned enhancement: escrow model holding funds in trust until milestone acceptance."},
      {label:"Document management — version control, approval workflow, collaboration",status:"planned",desc:"Project documents have: version history (every revised upload creates a new version, previous versions restorable), document approval workflow (submitted for review, approved or rejected with comments, locked once approved), inline comments and section-level annotations (similar to Google Docs), and collaborative editing for supported formats. Google Drive/Docs/Sheets/Slides integration links documents from Drive rather than uploading — always fetches the latest version. Image IP protection: uploaded images are watermarked and low-quality during bidding; full-resolution unlocked for the hired consultant only."},
      {label:"Consultant portal — discovery, bidding, and execution view",status:"planned",desc:"Consultants access a tailored portal showing: Discovery view (projects matching their expertise with name, category, budget range, deadline, required skills); Project detail view (full RFP after NDA acceptance); Proposal submission; and Execution view after being hired (their assigned tasks, milestone tracker, document repository, change requests, payment history, message threads). The portal's task dashboard shows all tasks assigned across all active projects, sorted by priority and deadline. Consultants see only their own proposals — never other consultants' bids or negotiation details."},
      {label:"Project owner dashboard — modular project control panel",status:"planned",desc:"The project dashboard shows: Phase progress bar (Startup → Closure with completion percentage per phase), open tasks by assignee and phase, risks and issues summary (counts by severity), budget gauge (spent vs allocated), milestone timeline (next 3 milestones with linked task completion), pending proposals count, pending change requests, pending payment requests, and recent document activity. Each widget links to the relevant section. The dashboard is the daily management surface — checked each morning to see what needs attention without opening every sub-section."},
      {label:"Knowledge base and lessons learned",status:"planned",desc:"At Closure phase, a Lessons Learned record is created: what went well, what went wrong, root causes, and recommendations. Lessons are tagged by project category, consultant, and topic. The Knowledge Base makes them searchable across all completed projects. When starting a new project of the same category, relevant lessons from past projects are surfaced for the owner to review at kick-off. Reusable assets (document templates, task lists, risk registers) can be saved as project templates and applied to new projects."},
      {label:"Role-based access control — four roles with scoped permissions",status:"planned",desc:"Four roles: Project Owner/Admin (full access — create projects, manage proposals, approve tasks, process payments, all documents and reports); Consultant (scoped — only projects they are invited to or hired for; submit proposals; view and complete assigned tasks; raise payment requests; access unlocked documents); Internal Team Member (view and manage tasks, documents, and risks but not approve proposals or trigger payments); Viewer/Stakeholder (read-only — project status, milestones, reports). Roles are per-project — the same person can be Admin on one and Viewer on another. Row-level security ensures consultants cannot see each other's proposals or negotiation details."},
      {label:"Reporting — project status, financial summary, consultant performance",status:"planned",desc:"Auto-generated reports from live data: Project Status Report (phase, task completion rate, risks/issues summary, milestone progress, budget — exportable as PDF); Financial Summary (total budget, amount paid, outstanding, cost by milestone and consultant — CSV export); Consultant Performance Report (on-time delivery rate, task completion rate, change requests raised, client rating — internal only by default). Reports can be scheduled for automatic generation and email delivery via the Automations section. Custom report builder selects which sections to include and in which order."}
    ]},
    {area:"Smart File Naming",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Template-based naming (Mode 1)",status:"planned",desc:"The most controlled option. The user defines or selects a naming pattern using field tokens such as [Date]_[Project]_[Type]_[Description]. The system applies the pattern to produce a structured filename. Possible template fields include: Date, Project, Client, Contact, Company, Document type, Category, Source, Short description, Version, Status, File type. Best for power users who want consistency and control."},
      {label:"Metadata + AI template matching (Mode 2)",status:"planned",desc:"The preferred smart default. The system reads the file and its metadata, then chooses the best naming template automatically. Example: user saves a screenshot, the system detects Project: Kitchen Renovation, Content: contractor quote, Company: ABC Plumbing, Date: March 2 2025, File type: PNG — then applies the best template and suggests 2025-03-02_KitchenReno_ContractorQuote_ABCPlumbing.png. This mode balances structure with intelligence — it still follows organized naming patterns, not random AI output."},
      {label:"Full AI naming (Mode 3)",status:"planned",desc:"The most automated option. No template is required. AI analyzes the content and generates a meaningful file name from scratch. Example: ABCPlumbing_KitchenRenovation_QuoteForSinkAndCabinetWork_2025-03-02.pdf. Best for users who do not want to manage naming patterns manually. The user can still accept, edit, or override the result."},
      {label:"User control — accept, edit, reject",status:"planned",desc:"The user always has final control. When the system suggests a name, the user can: accept it, edit it, reject it, choose another naming mode, set a default naming mode, or apply naming rules in bulk. Default behavior is configurable: always use metadata+AI template matching, always ask before renaming, auto-name screenshots, auto-name downloaded PDFs, use a project-specific naming template."},
      {label:"Project context enrichment",status:"planned",desc:"File naming becomes smarter when the file is connected to a Project Home. A file inside a project can inherit: project name, project category, project contacts, project status, project tags, related notes, and (later) related tasks and messages. This makes the filename more meaningful without the user manually typing everything."},
      {label:"OCR-driven naming",status:"planned",desc:"OCR is critical to the file naming system. For screenshots, PDFs, scanned documents, and images with text, the system extracts text and uses it to determine: document type, names, companies, dates, amounts, invoice numbers, quote numbers, addresses, event names, and topics. Example: a screenshot contains 'ABC Plumbing Estimate #3021' — the system infers Company: ABC Plumbing, Document type: Estimate, Reference number: 3021 and suggests 2025-03-02_ABCPlumbing_Estimate_3021.png."},
      {label:"AI summary-driven naming",status:"planned",desc:"AI summarization helps when the file content is not obvious from the filename. Example: original file download.pdf, AI summary 'Proposal from contractor for kitchen cabinet installation and plumbing modifications', suggested filename KitchenRenovation_CabinetPlumbingProposal_2025-03-02.pdf."},
      {label:"Contact and company detection",status:"planned",desc:"The system detects person and company names in file content and uses them in naming suggestions. Detected entities are cross-referenced against the Contacts module to use canonical names."},
      {label:"Duplicate content detection at save time",status:"planned",desc:"The system detects if a URL, image, or file has already been saved. This is different from the link-don't-duplicate architecture. Two related ideas: (A) Architectural deduplication — the same file exists once and is linked many times. (B) Duplicate content detection — the system recognizes when the user is saving the same content again. Examples: 'You already saved this URL three weeks ago.' 'This image already exists in Project X.' 'This PDF appears to match an existing file.' Helps prevent clutter and repeated captures."},
      {label:"Bulk rename",status:"planned",desc:"Power users need bulk control. Select multiple files and apply a naming template or AI rename to all of them in one operation. Example: select 50 screenshots from a project and apply [Date]_[Project]_[Type]_[ShortDescription]. A preview table shows original name to proposed name for each file before committing."},
      {label:"Bulk retag, assign, move, export, metadata update",status:"planned",desc:"Beyond bulk rename, the system supports: bulk retag, bulk assign to project, bulk move/link, bulk delete, bulk export, and bulk metadata update — all from the same multi-select interface."},
      {label:"Naming template library",status:"planned",desc:"Users can create, save, edit, and delete custom naming templates. The system provides at least 5 default templates: General, Invoice/Receipt, Screenshot, Project Document, Correspondence. Templates use field tokens like [Date], [Project], [Company], [DocType], [RefNumber], etc."},
      {label:"Rename history and revert",status:"planned",desc:"The system stores the original filename and all previous names with timestamps and naming mode used. User can revert to any previous name. All names in history are indexed for search."},
      {label:"Separator and case preferences",status:"planned",desc:"User can choose the separator character: underscore _ (default), hyphen -, period ., or space. Case normalization options: PascalCase (default), camelCase, lowercase, UPPERCASE, or original case. Date format configurable (default YYYY-MM-DD)."},
      {label:"Filename safety and length limits",status:"planned",desc:"Generated names are filesystem-safe: illegal characters replaced, 255-character max including extension, intelligent truncation preserving the most meaningful tokens, extension always preserved, no leading/trailing whitespace, consecutive separators collapsed."},
      {label:"Naming suggestions on existing files",status:"planned",desc:"User can trigger a naming suggestion on any existing file at any time, not only at capture time. Useful for fixing files saved months ago with meaningless names."}
    ]},
    {area:"Settings",color:"gray",items:[
      {label:"Full-page settings",status:"done",desc:"Settings is a first-class section navigated to via the gear icon in the nav bar, exactly like Projects or Calendar. It replaces the home content area entirely with a two-pane layout: a 200px category sidebar on the left and the selected settings panel on the right. This replaces the previous approach of a 740×520px modal overlay which felt too small for a growing settings surface."},
      {label:"Auto-save on change",status:"done",desc:"Every toggle, segment control, or dropdown in Settings writes directly to the prefs state object using setPrefs immediately when changed. There is no Save button to click and no Cancel to discard — changes apply instantly and the UI reflects them in real time (e.g. switching the header bar theme updates the actual header bar while you're looking at the Settings page)."},
      {label:"Features & Roadmap panel",status:"done",desc:"This panel — the one you are reading now — tracks every significant feature across all sections of Plenix. Each feature has a label, a status badge (Done / Planned / In Progress), and a detailed explanation in a drawer. Features are grouped by product area. The done/total count badge next to each area heading gives an at-a-glance completion ratio. This panel grows automatically as new features are added to the FEATURES data array."},
      {label:"Dark mode",status:"planned",desc:"A full dark theme is planned. All CSS custom properties (--color-gray-100, --color-white, --color-blue-400, --color-black, etc.) are already defined as variables on :root, which means flipping to dark mode requires only overriding those variables on a [data-theme='dark'] selector. The groundwork is done — the implementation is a CSS authoring task, not an architectural one."},
      {label:"Keyboard shortcuts reference",status:"planned",desc:"A dedicated panel listing all keyboard shortcuts in the app: Ctrl K for search/command palette, Escape to close modals, arrow keys for list navigation, and section-specific shortcuts (e.g. N for new note, T for new task). Will be accessible both from Settings and from a help icon in the header."}
    ]},
    {area:"Tasks",color:"green",items:[
      {label:"Task list — core row anatomy",status:"done",desc:"Each task row shows: a checkbox (toggles completion, adds strikethrough and fades to 60% opacity), a priority color dot (red for urgent, orange for high, yellow for medium, gray for low), the task title, a project label badge, and a due date in monospace. Checking a task updates the progress bar on the associated project detail view in real time."},
      {label:"Task structure — rich metadata object",status:"planned",desc:"Each task is a structured object that can carry: Priority level (Urgent / High / Medium / Low — four levels, color-coded); Due date with optional time (e.g. March 10 at 2:30 PM — the time makes it schedulable on the day timeline); Subtasks (nested checklist); Labels and tags (custom, color-coded, multi-value); Comments (threaded discussion history); Notes (a formatted text area for context); Time estimate; Status (Not started / In progress / Blocked / Done / Canceled); and links to related projects, calendar events, habits, goals, or journal entries. Tasks can exist independently or as part of a project workflow. All metadata is optional — a task can be as simple as a title with a checkbox."},
      {label:"Four-level priority system — Urgent, High, Medium, Low",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks support four priority levels rather than three: Urgent (red — time-critical, needs immediate action), High (orange — important but not on fire), Medium (yellow — standard work), and Low (gray — nice to do, no deadline pressure). The four levels allow finer-grained sorting and filtering. The priority dot is visible on every task row, in the Kanban card, and on the Calendar timeline block. Auto-sort rule: Urgent tasks always surface above High, which surfaces above Medium and Low, regardless of due date. The priority level is also used by the AI day planner to assign earlier time slots to higher-priority tasks."},
      {label:"Due dates with optional time and overdue tracking",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks can have a due date (date only) or a due date + time (specific deadline). Date-only tasks appear as all-day markers on the Calendar. Tasks with a time appear as a schedulable block on the day timeline. Overdue detection: at midnight, any incomplete task whose due date has passed is automatically flagged as overdue. Overdue tasks appear with a red date indicator in the task list, in the Tasks dashboard widget's Overdue section, and in the Today view of the Calendar. The overdue indicator persists until the task is completed or the due date is updated. Overdue tasks are sorted above other tasks in the active list regardless of priority."},
      {label:"Task organization — lists, areas, and projects",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks are organized at three levels. Areas of responsibility are top-level containers (Work, Personal, Health, Finance). Within an area, Projects or goals collect related tasks. Individual tasks sit within a project. The sidebar reflects this three-level hierarchy with count badges. A task can belong to one project but be visible across multiple views (Today, Due Soon, All Tasks) simultaneously — its organizational home and its temporal urgency are independent."},
      {label:"Priority-based workflow — Current vs Future",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks are separated into two buckets: Current (active focus — what you are working on today or this week) and Future (deferred items — acknowledged but not yet acted upon). The user manually moves tasks between Current and Future, keeping the active list short and focused. Tasks can also be scheduled to move from Future to Current on a specific date, surfacing automatically when the time comes."},
      {label:"Multiple workflow views",status:"planned",desc:"Four views of the same underlying task data: Priority list (ordered, drag to re-prioritize); Kanban board (columns by status, cards draggable between stages); Date-based planning (calendar timeline showing tasks by due date); Categorization matrix (2x2 urgency/importance Eisenhower grid). Switching views is a presentation change, not a data change."},
      {label:"Subtasks and task hierarchy",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks can contain subtasks — nested checklist items with their own checkboxes, priority, and due dates. The parent task shows a progress indicator (e.g. 3/5 subtasks done). Subtasks can be promoted to full tasks. Up to three levels of nesting (task → subtask → sub-subtask). The task detail panel shows the full subtask list, notes, attributes, and activity log."},
      {label:"Labels and tags — color-coded cross-workflow categorization",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks can be assigned one or more custom labels. Each label has a name and a color. Labels are user-defined and reusable across all tasks and projects — not scoped to a single project. Common uses: 'client', 'waiting', 'admin', 'deep-work', 'quick-win'. Labels appear as colored pills on task cards and rows. Filtering by label in the sidebar or toolbar instantly shows all tasks with that label regardless of which project they belong to. Labels are distinct from project membership — a task has one project but can have many labels."},
      {label:"Threaded comments — discussion history per task",status:"planned",desc:"Each task has a Comments tab in its detail panel. Comments are threaded — a comment can have replies forming a sub-thread. Each comment shows the author, timestamp, and formatted text (supports bold, italic, inline code, and links). Comments are used to store: decisions made about the task, questions and answers between team members, status updates that are not reflected in the task fields themselves, and references to related information. The comment history is immutable — comments can be edited within a short window after posting but not deleted after that, preserving a reliable audit trail. Comments are separate from the task's Notes field (which is a personal scratchpad) — comments are for shared discussion."},
      {label:"Task detail panel — full metadata view",status:"planned",desc:"Clicking any task row opens the task detail panel as a right-side drawer. The panel shows all task fields: title (editable), priority, status, due date + time, time estimate, project, labels, assignee (team mode), subtask list with progress, Notes (formatted text area), Comments (threaded), and an Activity log (auto-generated history of all changes: who changed what field, when). The panel is open while the task list remains scrollable behind it — the user does not lose their place in the list. All fields are editable inline within the panel."},
      {label:"Workflow interaction — move between stages, drag-and-drop",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks can be moved between workflow stages (Kanban columns) by dragging the card or by changing the Status field in the task detail panel. Within a column, tasks can be reordered by dragging. In the list view, tasks can be reordered by dragging rows within their group. Reordering updates the manual sort order, which is preserved independently of any sort setting. Marking a task as complete (checkbox or 'Done' status) moves it to the completed state and removes it from the active list unless 'Show completed tasks' is enabled in Settings."},
      {label:"Calendar and habit integration",status:"planned",desc:"Tasks with due dates appear directly on the Calendar timeline alongside events — the user sees their full commitment picture in one view. Tasks with a specific time appear as a schedulable block. Tasks can also be linked to habits (e.g. a daily exercise task that is also a tracked habit) or goals (the task is a milestone toward a defined goal). When a habit-linked task is completed, it logs the habit completion for that day simultaneously. When a goal-linked task is completed, the goal progress updates automatically."},
      {label:"Task capture — quick add, voice, email, integrations",status:"planned",desc:"Quick add: persistent input at top of any task list, natural language date parsing. Voice: microphone button in Capture flow. Email: forwarding to a Plenix capture address creates a task with subject as title, body as notes, sender linked to contact. Integrations: GitHub issues, Asana, Google Tasks via Connectors with bidirectional status. All captured tasks land in an Inbox for triage before entering the active system."},
      {label:"Reminders and scheduling",status:"planned",desc:"Each task can have multiple reminders: specific date+time, relative to due date ('2 hours before'), or location-based (future). Fires as in-app and browser notifications. Scheduling view shows tasks on a timeline to redistribute load by dragging. Over-committed days highlighted in orange."},
      {label:"Sidebar filters and grouping",status:"planned",desc:"Sidebar items: All Tasks, Today, Due Soon (7 days), By Project, By Area, Flagged, Completed. Each is a live query. Toolbar grouping: by project, by priority, by due date, or flat. Active filter and grouping remembered per session."},
      {label:"Sidebar filter → task list live connection",status:"done",desc:"Clicking any sidebar item filters the main task list to that scope. Active filter shown as highlighted sidebar row and label above list. Clearing returns to All Tasks."},
      {label:"Automation and API",status:"planned",desc:"Automations create tasks from triggers (new client email, GitHub PR assignment, Monday morning review prompt). API allows any external system to POST a task. Zapier and Make connectors planned. Captured tasks land in the Inbox for triage."}
    ]},
    {area:"Text Templates & Snippets",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Snippet library — save any text as a reusable template",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user types the same phrases, responses, email openers, signatures, product descriptions, and RFQ paragraphs dozens of times per week. Instead of retyping or copy-pasting from old documents, they define a snippet once and insert it anywhere with a short shortcut. The Snippet Library is a collection of named text templates, each assigned a keyboard shortcut trigger (e.g. /ty for a thank-you sentence, /rfq for a full RFQ email template, /sig for an email signature, /addr for a shipping address). Snippets can be plain text or rich text (with formatting preserved when inserted into a rich-text field). They are organized into folders (Personal, Work, Client Communication, Procurement, Support) for browsability. Each snippet shows a preview of its content and its shortcut. Snippets are created from a library panel in Plenix Settings → Templates, or on-the-fly by selecting text and choosing 'Save as snippet'."},
      {label:"Text expansion — type shortcut, text inserts instantly",status:"planned",desc:"When the user types a defined shortcut (e.g. /rfq) in any text field in Plenix — a note, an email compose window, a WhatsApp message, a task description, a database cell — the shortcut is detected and replaced immediately with the snippet's full content. The replacement is instant and invisible — the user types the shortcut, it disappears, and the expanded text appears as if typed at full speed. The trigger character is configurable: /shortcut (default, using forward slash as a namespace), ;shortcut, or any other prefix the user sets. The expansion happens without any modal or confirmation — just type and it expands. If the user types the shortcut but does not want to expand it (rare), pressing Escape before the expansion fires cancels it. This is the core Text Blaze / TextExpander interaction model applied natively within Plenix."},
      {label:"Dynamic templates — form fields and variable placeholders",status:"planned",desc:"Static snippets expand the same text every time. Dynamic templates include interactive form fields that prompt the user to fill in values at the time of insertion. A template like 'Dear {name}, thank you for your enquiry about {product}...' pops up a small inline form with fields for Name and Product when the shortcut is triggered. The user fills in the fields and presses Enter — the completed text inserts with the variables substituted. Field types: text input (single-line or multi-line), dropdown (pre-defined options — e.g. choose from a list of product categories), date picker (inserts a formatted date), and checkbox (inserts a conditional phrase — 'If checked, add the urgency note'). Fields can have default values. This makes it possible to have a single RFQ template that handles dozens of different supplier and product combinations without creating a separate snippet for each."},
      {label:"Dynamic formulas — dates, calculations, and system values",status:"planned",desc:"Snippets can include formula commands that compute their value at the moment of insertion: {date} inserts today's date in the configured format (e.g. '25 April 2026'), {date+7} inserts a date 7 days from now (useful for payment terms: 'Payment due by {date+30}'), {time} inserts the current time, {day} inserts the day of the week, {clipboard} inserts the current clipboard contents (useful for 'Following up on your message: {clipboard}' patterns), {cursor} sets the cursor position after insertion (the cursor lands at a designated spot in the expanded text so the user can continue typing immediately). Formulas eliminate the most common manual adjustments users make after expanding a template — today's date, a future deadline, a reference number from the clipboard."},
      {label:"System-wide expansion — works everywhere in Plenix",status:"planned",desc:"Text expansion works in every text input across Plenix without needing to be enabled per-section: Notes editor, Document editor, Email compose, WhatsApp message compose, Task title and description, Calendar event title and description, Database cells, Contact notes, AI Chat input, Search bar, and any other text field. The expansion engine hooks into the global keyboard input handler so it is a single system — not a per-section implementation. A future desktop-app version will also expand snippets in external applications (Gmail in the browser, Google Docs, LinkedIn, Slack, any desktop app) similar to how Text Blaze works as a Chrome extension or macOS/Windows app."},
      {label:"Shared snippet folders — team templates",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A team uses the same email responses, product descriptions, and support replies. Instead of each person maintaining their own snippet library, a shared folder allows one person to create and maintain templates that everyone on the team can use. Shared folders are created in the Snippet Library and shared with specific Plenix users or made available to everyone in a workspace. When the owner updates a snippet in the shared folder, all users immediately get the updated version — no redistribution needed. Each shared snippet shows who created it and when it was last updated. Read-only sharing (use but not edit) and collaborative sharing (any member can edit) are configurable per folder. This makes the snippet library a living team knowledge base for communication standards."},
      {label:"Snippet search and quick-insert panel",status:"planned",desc:"A quick-insert panel (triggered by a keyboard shortcut, e.g. Ctrl+Shift+V or a dedicated button in any toolbar) opens a searchable list of all snippets. The user types to filter by snippet name, folder, or content. Clicking a result or pressing Enter inserts the snippet at the current cursor position. This is the mouse-driven alternative to typing the shortcut — useful when the user cannot remember the exact shortcut but knows roughly what they are looking for. The panel shows the snippet name, its shortcut, and a preview of the first line of content. Frequently used snippets are surfaced at the top of the list regardless of alphabetical order."},
      {label:"Snippet analytics — usage tracking",status:"planned",desc:"The Snippet Library shows usage data per snippet: how many times it has been expanded in the last 7 days, 30 days, and all time. A summary statistic estimates the total characters saved (expansions × average snippet length) and converts that to an estimated time saved (at a configured typing speed, default 200 characters per minute). Snippets with zero uses in 90 days are flagged as stale — the user can decide to delete them or update them. Frequently-used snippets are highlighted so the user knows which templates are pulling their weight. For team shared folders, per-member usage data shows who is using the shared templates and who is not."}
    ]},
    {area:"Time Tracking",color:"brown",items:[
      {label:"Live timer — start/stop with project and task association",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user starts working on a task and wants to record exactly how long they spend on it — for billing, for self-awareness, or for project estimation. A global timer button is accessible from the header bar or from any task's detail panel. Clicking Start begins a live timer. The timer runs continuously and is visible as an elapsed time display (HH:MM:SS) in the header. Clicking Stop records the entry with the elapsed duration. During tracking, the user can associate the timer with a project and/or a specific task — or leave it unassociated and categorize it later. The timer continues running if the user navigates to a different section of Plenix. If the app is closed accidentally, the timer state is recovered from local storage on next open. Only one timer can run at a time."},
      {label:"Manual time entry — retrospective logging",status:"planned",desc:"Time entries can be added manually for time not captured by the live timer — time spent in meetings, on calls, or work done before the user started tracking. A manual entry panel collects: Start time (date + time), End time (date + time — duration is calculated automatically), Description (optional free-text), Project association, Task association, and Billable status (toggle with hourly rate field). Manual entries appear in the same time log as live-tracked entries. Editing a live-recorded entry is also done via this panel — the user can adjust the start/end times, add a description, or change the project association after the fact."},
      {label:"Billable time — hourly rates and billing tracking",status:"planned",desc:"Each time entry can be marked as billable or non-billable. Billable entries can have an hourly rate assigned — either inherited from the associated project (set at the project level) or overridden per entry. The time log shows a calculated value for each billable entry (duration × hourly rate). Aggregate views show total billable hours and total billable value for a project, a date range, or a client. This supports freelancers and agencies who need to track invoiceable time across multiple clients and projects. A 'Mark as invoiced' action on a set of entries flags them so they do not appear in future unbilled totals, maintaining an accurate running balance of what has been billed and what is outstanding."},
      {label:"Weekly and monthly views — time distribution",status:"planned",desc:"The Time Tracking section has two aggregate views. Weekly view: a horizontal bar chart showing total tracked time per day across the current week (Mon–Sun). Each bar is color-segmented by project, so the user can see not just how much time was tracked each day but how it was distributed across projects. A total for the week is shown alongside a billable/non-billable split. Monthly view: same structure but across the full month, with weekly subtotals. Both views include a table of individual time entries below the chart so the user can drill into the detail behind any bar. Date navigation (previous/next week or month) is available from the toolbar."},
      {label:"Statistics and reporting — totals, distribution, trends",status:"planned",desc:"The Reports view in Time Tracking provides: Total tracked time (for any configurable date range), Billable vs non-billable split (hours and percentage), Time by project (a pie or bar chart showing what proportion of total time went to each project), Time by task (within a selected project), Daily average (total time divided by working days in the range), and Comparison to previous period (e.g. this week vs last week, this month vs last month). Reports can be filtered by project, by billable status, and by date range. A CSV export button downloads the filtered time entries as a spreadsheet for use in invoicing tools or external reporting. The Automations section can be configured to generate a Weekly Time Report note automatically every Friday."},
      {label:"Integration with tasks, projects, calendar, and Pomodoro timer",status:"planned",desc:"Time entries are bidirectionally linked to tasks and projects. When a task has time entries, the task detail panel shows the total time logged against it. When a project has time entries, the project detail shows total tracked time alongside task completion progress — giving a dual view of effort (time) and output (tasks done). The Calendar integration works in both directions: time blocks placed on the Calendar timeline can be converted to time entries (confirming that the planned time was actually worked), and time entries can be shown as blocks on the Calendar for a visual record of how the day was actually spent. The Pomodoro timer in the Calendar section creates time entries automatically — each completed Pomodoro is logged as a 25-minute entry against the active task."}
    ]},
    {area:"Translation & Dictionary",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"In-app translation — select text and translate anywhere",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user is reading a supplier email in Arabic, reviewing a contract clause in French, or composing a WhatsApp message to a contact who speaks Spanish. Rather than copying text to a separate app, Plenix provides inline translation accessible from anywhere text appears. Selecting any text in Plenix — in a note, an email, a message thread, a task description, a database cell — surfaces a contextual action bar with a Translate option. The translation appears immediately below the selection in a tooltip or inline panel, showing the detected source language and the translated text. The user can replace the original with the translation, copy the translation, or dismiss it. The target language defaults to the user's interface language but can be changed per translation or set permanently in Settings."},
      {label:"Google Translate integration",status:"planned",desc:"Google Translate is the primary translation engine for broad language coverage. Plenix connects via the Google Cloud Translation API (v3), which supports 130+ languages with automatic source language detection. Used for: translating selected text inline, translating entire notes or email bodies, translating incoming WhatsApp messages in the Unified Inbox (each message can show a 'Show translation' toggle), and translating file contents (PDFs, documents). The Google Translate connector is configured in Settings → Connectors with a Google Cloud API key. For users without an API key, a fallback uses the public Google Translate endpoint for limited volume. The API returns the translated text and the detected source language, both displayed in the translation panel."},
      {label:"AI translation — GPT and Claude for nuanced, context-aware output",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Google Translate produces accurate literal translations but sometimes misses tone, idiom, and context — especially for business communication, legal text, or creative writing. Plenix offers AI-powered translation using GPT (OpenAI) or Claude (Anthropic) as an alternative or complement to Google Translate. AI translation is invoked with a prompt that includes context: 'Translate the following [source language] text to [target language]. The context is a [professional email / WhatsApp message / legal clause / product description]. Preserve tone and adapt idioms naturally.' The output is more natural-sounding for human communication. The user can toggle between Google Translate (faster, more literal) and AI translation (slower, more nuanced) per translation or set a default in Settings. AI translation uses the same model and API key already configured for the AI assistant — no additional setup required."},
      {label:"WordReference Dictionary — definitions, conjugations, forum results",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user is composing in a second language and wants to verify a word choice, check its gender (in Spanish/French/Italian), see conjugation tables, or understand subtle usage differences. WordReference is the gold-standard multilingual dictionary for European languages. Plenix integrates WordReference lookup: double-clicking a foreign-language word (or selecting it and choosing 'Look up') opens a dictionary panel showing the word's definitions, part of speech, gender, example sentences, and — uniquely to WordReference — a curated set of forum discussion results where native speakers have debated the correct usage of that exact word or phrase. Supported language pairs include Spanish, French, Italian, Portuguese, German, English, and others. The WordReference panel appears as a side drawer so the user can continue composing while consulting the dictionary."},
      {label:"iTranslate — conversation and voice translation",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user is on a voice or video call with a supplier, client, or partner who speaks a different language, or needs to translate spoken content in real time. iTranslate (itranslate.com) provides conversation-mode translation — two people speaking in different languages, with the app translating each utterance in real time. Plenix integrates the iTranslate API for: voice-to-translated-text (speak in one language, receive translated text in another — distinct from Superwhisper's transcription which outputs in the same language), conversation mode in the AI Chat section (useful for bilingual meetings), and transliterating text between scripts (e.g. Arabic ↔ Latin romanisation). iTranslate supports 100+ languages and 40+ text-to-speech voices for reading translations aloud."},
      {label:"Unified Inbox — auto-translate incoming messages",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Messages arrive in the Unified Inbox from contacts who write in different languages. The user needs to understand the content without manually triggering a translation on each one. Each message in the Unified Inbox (WhatsApp, email, Instagram DM, etc.) that is detected as a non-preferred language shows a 'Translate' button inline. Tapping it expands the translated version beneath the original. A 'Always translate from [language]' option in the inbox settings auto-translates all future messages from that language without requiring a tap. The source language and translation engine are shown as a small label ('Translated from Arabic · Google'). The reply composer also offers a 'Compose in [language] and translate before sending' mode — the user writes naturally in their language and the outbound message is translated before delivery."},
      {label:"Note and document translation — full-body translate",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user receives a supplier contract, a product specification, or a research document in a foreign language and needs a working translation of the entire content. In the Notes section, a 'Translate note' action in the note toolbar sends the full text to the selected translation engine and creates a new note titled '[Original title] — Translated' containing the output, preserving the original's formatting (headings, bullets, tables). In the Files section, PDFs and documents can be submitted for full-document translation — the translated version is saved as a new file alongside the original. Both the original and translated versions are linked in their metadata so the user can compare them. For long documents, translation is processed in sections to stay within API token limits."},
      {label:"Translation settings — engine preference, target language, glossary",status:"planned",desc:"Settings → Translation covers: Default engine (Google Translate / GPT / Claude / iTranslate — selectable globally or per context); Default target language (the language all translations output to, defaults to interface language); Auto-detect source language (on by default); Custom glossary (user-defined term pairs that override the translation engine — e.g. 'always translate [company name] as [X]', or 'keep the word RFQ untranslated'); API key management for Google Cloud Translation and iTranslate; and a Translation history log (all translations performed, searchable, useful for auditing or re-using translated content). The glossary is particularly important for procurement and business contexts where product names, technical terms, and brand names must be rendered consistently regardless of which engine is used."}
    ]},
    {area:"Unified Inbox",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"Single inbox — all channels, one view",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user is active across multiple communication surfaces — WhatsApp, email, Instagram DMs, Twitter/X mentions, LinkedIn messages, Telegram, Discord, Facebook comments, and more. Currently they must open each app separately, check each inbox, and mentally track what needs a response and what has been handled. Plenix's Unified Inbox funnels every inbound interaction from every connected channel into a single chronological or priority-sorted feed. The user opens one screen and sees everything: a WhatsApp message from a client, an email from a supplier, a Twitter mention, an Instagram story reaction, a LinkedIn connection request, a Telegram bot message. Each item shows the source channel (color-coded icon), the sender's name and avatar, a preview of the message or interaction, the time received, and a read/unread state. The user can reply directly from the inbox without switching apps or opening a separate thread view. This is the operational hub for anyone managing communication across multiple platforms."},
      {label:"Interaction types — messages, mentions, reactions, comments, emails",status:"planned",desc:"The Unified Inbox aggregates every type of inbound interaction, not just direct messages. Supported interaction types: Direct messages (WhatsApp, Telegram, Instagram DM, Twitter/X DM, Facebook Messenger, LinkedIn message, Discord DM); Email (Gmail, Outlook — full threads, not just notifications); Mentions (Twitter/X @mentions, Instagram tags in posts and stories, LinkedIn mentions, Reddit username mentions); Comments (Instagram post comments, Facebook post comments, YouTube video comments); Reactions (Instagram story reactions, Facebook post reactions — someone reacted to your content); Notifications (GitHub PR review requests, Asana task assignments, Slack @mentions). Each interaction type has its own visual indicator — a reply bubble for messages, an @ symbol for mentions, a heart for reactions, a speech bubble for comments — so the user can scan the inbox type at a glance without reading every item."},
      {label:"Inbox filters and views — channel, type, assignee, status",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A busy inbox with hundreds of interactions needs to be manageable. The Unified Inbox supports multiple filter dimensions: by channel (show only WhatsApp, or only email, or only social mentions); by interaction type (messages only, mentions only, comments only); by status (Unread, Needs reply, Replied, Resolved, Snoozed); by assignee (in team mode — show only interactions assigned to me, or to a specific team member); by contact (show all interactions from a specific person across all channels); by date range; and by keyword search across all message content. Filter combinations are saveable as named views (e.g. 'Client WhatsApps', 'Unresolved mentions', 'Team queue') that appear as tabs at the top of the inbox for quick switching. The default view shows everything unread, sorted by most recent."},
      {label:"Reply from inbox — channel-native responses",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user sees a WhatsApp message and an Instagram comment in the same inbox and wants to reply to both without opening WhatsApp or Instagram separately. Each inbox item has an inline reply panel that opens below it. The reply is sent via the correct API for that channel — a WhatsApp reply goes via the WhatsApp Cloud API, an Instagram comment reply goes via the Instagram Graph API, a Twitter mention reply goes via the Twitter API — but the user just types their response in one consistent composer. The composer is context-aware: for WhatsApp it shows the option to use a message template (required for first-contact messages outside the 24h window); for email it shows a full reply editor with subject threading; for social comments it shows a plain text field with character count. Sent replies are marked as Resolved in the inbox automatically unless the user marks them otherwise."},
      {label:"Personalized customer service — contact context in inbox",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: When a message arrives in the inbox, the user needs context immediately — who is this person, what's the history, are they a client or a prospect, have they had issues before? Every inbox item shows a contact card panel on the right:the sender's full contact record from Plenix's Contacts section (name, company, role, tags), their interaction history across all channels (previous WhatsApp messages, emails, mentions — not just the current channel), any notes previously added by the user, any open tasks linked to this contact, and any projects they are associated with. If the sender is not yet in Contacts, a one-click 'Add to Contacts' button creates a record pre-filled with what Plenix already knows (name from the platform, handle, profile image). This eliminates the need to context-switch to CRM or email history while composing a reply."},
      {label:"Team collaboration — assign, comment, and hand off",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A team where multiple people manage communications needs to coordinate without duplicating responses or dropping items. The Unified Inbox supports team workflows: Assign — any inbox item can be assigned to a specific team member; they receive a notification and the item appears in their personal queue; the inbox shows who each item is assigned to. Internal comments — team members can leave internal notes on any inbox item (visible only to the team, not the external sender) to add context, ask questions, or brief the assignee ('This is the client from Project X — they're unhappy about the delay'). Collision detection — if two team members open the same inbox item at the same time, a 'Someone is viewing this' indicator prevents duplicate replies. Status tracking — items move through statuses (New → In Progress → Resolved) with timestamps so the team can see response times and workload distribution. SLA indicators show items that have been waiting too long without a response."},
      {label:"Snooze, reminders, and follow-up scheduling",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Not every message needs an immediate reply — some need to be acted on later. The user can snooze any inbox item to a specific time (e.g. 'remind me tomorrow at 9 AM') or a relative delay (e.g. 'in 3 hours'). Snoozed items disappear from the inbox and reappear at the scheduled time as if they just arrived — with a snooze badge so the user knows it was deferred. A separate 'Scheduled follow-ups' view lists all snoozed items. Additionally, the user can set a follow-up reminder on a sent message ('if I don't get a reply in 48 hours, remind me to follow up') — Plenix monitors for a reply and triggers the reminder only if no response is received by the deadline. This prevents hot leads, supplier RFQs, and client questions from silently falling through the cracks."},
      {label:"Inbox analytics — response times, volume, channel breakdown",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user or team lead wants to understand communication patterns — how fast are we responding, which channels generate the most inbound, are we keeping up with volume? The Inbox Analytics view shows: average first response time per channel, median resolution time, inbound volume by channel and by day/week/month, breakdown by interaction type (messages vs mentions vs comments), top senders by volume, and unresolved item age distribution. For teams, it shows per-member response time and resolved count. All metrics are filterable by date range and channel. The data helps the user identify which channels need more attention, at what times of day inbound volume peaks, and whether response time targets are being met."}
    ]},
    {area:"Voice Input",color:"blue",items:[
      {label:"System-wide voice dictation — type anywhere by speaking",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to dictate text into any field in Plenix — a task title, a note, a WhatsApp message, a search bar, an email reply — without clicking a microphone button on each individual field. A global hotkey (configurable, e.g. Option+Space or a push-to-talk key) activates voice input from anywhere in the app. The user speaks, and transcribed text is inserted at the current cursor position. No field needs to be specifically voice-enabled — the system captures audio, transcribes it, and simulates keyboard input at the active cursor. This is the Superwhisper model applied natively within Plenix: voice as a universal input method rather than a per-feature bolt-on. The hotkey can be held for push-to-talk (release to transcribe) or tapped to toggle recording on/off."},
      {label:"Context-aware AI modes — output adapts to what you are doing",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Raw transcription produces a wall of unpunctuated words. The same spoken content should be formatted differently depending on where it is going: a voice note should be structured as a note with paragraphs; a task title should be short and imperative; a WhatsApp message should be casual and concise; an email should be properly structured with greeting and sign-off. Plenix's voice input uses context-aware AI modes that detect the active section and format the output accordingly. Modes: Note dictation (structured paragraphs, markdown headings if speaking section titles); Task capture (extracts action item, due date, priority from natural speech — 'remind me to follow up with the supplier on Friday high priority' → task titled 'Follow up with supplier', due Friday, priority High); Email draft (greeting, body, sign-off, professional tone); Message (casual, concise, appropriate for chat); and a Raw mode (pure transcription, no AI processing). The user can also create custom modes with a system prompt that transforms the dictation in any way they define."},
      {label:"Offline and on-device processing — privacy-first transcription",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user does not want their voice recordings sent to a cloud server for transcription — especially when dictating sensitive business information, personal notes, or private messages. Plenix supports fully offline voice transcription using on-device AI models based on OpenAI's Whisper (specifically the whisper.cpp implementation, which is optimized for Apple Silicon and runs at near-real-time speed without an internet connection). The user chooses their processing preference: Local (audio never leaves the device, no internet required, smaller model, slightly lower accuracy) or Cloud (audio sent to a transcription API — OpenAI Whisper, Deepgram, or Groq — for higher accuracy and faster processing on longer recordings). The local option is the default for privacy. Cloud mode is opt-in and clearly labeled. Model tiers mirror Superwhisper's approach: Nano/Fast (local, instant), Standard (local, balanced), Pro/Ultra (cloud, highest accuracy)."},
      {label:"100+ language support with automatic detection and translation",status:"planned",desc:"Voice input supports transcription in over 100 languages with automatic language detection — the user does not need to specify which language they are speaking. The system detects the language from the first few words and transcribes accordingly. For multilingual users: the user can switch mid-sentence between languages and the system handles it. A translation mode allows the user to dictate in one language and have the output appear in another (e.g. dictate in Arabic, receive English text) — useful for drafting communications in a second language or translating field notes on the fly. Language preference can be pinned per mode (e.g. 'always transcribe Note mode in English, but Message mode follows the detected language')."},
      {label:"Audio and video file transcription",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user has a recorded meeting, a voice note from their phone, a supplier call recording, or a video interview and needs the text. Beyond live dictation, Plenix accepts uploaded audio and video files for transcription. Supported formats: MP3, M4A, OGG, WAV, MP4, MOV, WEBM. The file is processed by the same transcription engine (local or cloud, user's choice) and the output is delivered as a Note in Plenix, timestamped and linked to the source file. Speaker diarisation (distinguishing between speakers and labeling their turns) is available when using cloud processing. Transcribed files are searchable — finding a word surfaces the exact timestamp in the original recording."},
      {label:"Recording history — searchable, replayable",status:"planned",desc:"Every voice dictation session is stored in a Recording History log accessible from the Voice Input settings. Each entry shows the date, duration, the mode used, the raw transcript, and the final AI-processed output. The user can: replay the original audio, edit the transcript if the AI misheard something, re-run the AI processing with a different mode, copy the output, or delete the recording. The history is searchable by content — typing a word finds all recordings that contained it. This is useful for recovering a dictation that wasn't pasted correctly, reviewing what was captured during a walk, or auditing past voice notes. History can be cleared on a rolling schedule (e.g. keep 30 days) or manually."},
      {label:"Custom modes — user-defined voice transformation prompts",status:"planned",desc:"Beyond the built-in context modes, the user can create custom modes with a name, a trigger (manual selection, or automatic when a specific section is active), and a system prompt that defines how the AI should transform the dictation. Examples: 'Morning Standup' mode (prompt: 'Convert this voice note into a bulleted standup update with Yesterday, Today, and Blockers sections'); 'Supplier Note' mode (prompt: 'Extract key details from this voice note: supplier name, product discussed, price mentioned, follow-up action'); 'Brain Dump to Tasks' mode (prompt: 'Convert this stream-of-consciousness voice note into a list of actionable tasks, each starting with a verb'). Custom modes use the same AI pipeline as the rest of Plenix. The user can bring their own API key for the language model step if they want to use a specific provider or model. Modes are shareable as JSON exports."},
      {label:"Voice capture into Capture inbox",status:"planned",desc:"The Capture button in the Plenix header includes a voice option — pressing it and speaking creates a new item in the Capture inbox (the triage zone for all incoming tasks, notes, and ideas). The voice content is processed by the active mode (default: a general capture mode that extracts tasks, notes, and ideas from natural speech and structures them appropriately). A spoken capture like 'I need to call Marcus about the contract by Thursday, and I had an idea for the packaging design — maybe use a matte black finish' produces two Capture items: a task ('Call Marcus about contract', due Thursday) and a note ('Packaging idea: matte black finish'). The user reviews and triages these items from the Capture inbox before they flow into Tasks or Notes."}
    ]},
    {area:"WhatsApp Integration",color:"green",items:[
      {label:"WhatsApp Cloud API — native send and receive",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to send and receive WhatsApp messages directly from Plenix — to contacts, clients, and suppliers — without switching to a separate app. Plenix integrates with the WhatsApp Cloud API (Meta's official API, v25.0) rather than unofficial automation tools. The user connects their WhatsApp Business Account by providing a Phone Number ID and a permanent access token from the Meta Developer portal. Outbound messages are sent via POST to the /messages endpoint on the business phone number. Inbound messages are received via a webhook that Plenix registers with Meta. Once connected, WhatsApp conversations appear in Plenix's Messaging section alongside other channels. All messages are stored in Plenix's thread model — the same thread view used for email and other messaging channels. This is the foundation layer on which all other WhatsApp features are built."},
      {label:"Message types — text, media, audio, documents, location, reactions",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: WhatsApp conversations involve more than plain text — the user needs to send voice notes, images, documents, location pins, and react to messages. Plenix supports all core WhatsApp Cloud API message types: Text messages (plain and with URL preview); Image messages (JPEG/PNG, with optional caption); Audio messages (MP3, OGG — sent as voice notes; the recipient hears them as native WhatsApp voice messages); Video messages (MP4, with caption); Document messages (PDF, DOCX, XLSX, etc. — displayed with filename and file type icon); Sticker messages (WebP format); Location messages (latitude, longitude, name, and address — renders as a map pin in the recipient's chat); Contact card messages (vCard format); Reaction messages (emoji reactions to a specific message, referencing the message ID). Each type maps to a specific API payload structure. File uploads are handled by first uploading media to the Meta media endpoint to obtain a media ID, then referencing that ID in the message payload."},
      {label:"Message templates — HSM for business-initiated conversations",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: WhatsApp requires pre-approved message templates (called HSMs — Highly Structured Messages) for any business-initiated conversation outside the 24-hour customer service window. The user needs to create, submit for approval, and send these templates from within Plenix without touching the Graph API directly. Plenix's template manager connects to the WhatsApp Business Account via the Graph API (v25.0 /message_templates endpoint) to: list all existing templates with their approval status (APPROVED, PENDING, REJECTED), create new templates with a name, language, category (MARKETING, UTILITY, AUTHENTICATION), and structured components, submit templates to Meta for review, and delete templates that are no longer needed. Template components include: Header (TEXT, IMAGE, VIDEO, or DOCUMENT — sets the top section of the message), Body (the main message text with variable placeholders in {{1}} format), Footer (optional small text at the bottom), and Buttons (QUICK_REPLY buttons or CALL_TO_ACTION buttons linking to a URL or phone number). When sending a template, variable values are passed in the components array to fill the placeholders dynamically (e.g. customer name, order number, tracking link)."},
      {label:"Template variable personalization — dynamic placeholders",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: A template like 'Hello {{1}}, your order {{2}} is ready for collection' needs to be personalized for each recipient before sending. Plenix's template sending flow shows the template with its placeholders highlighted and lets the user fill each variable from: manual text input, a contact field (first name, company name, phone), a project field, a task field, or a custom data source. For bulk sends (e.g. sending the same template to a list of contacts), Plenix maps each placeholder to a column in the contact list so every message is personalized automatically. A preview panel shows the rendered message for each recipient before sending. This eliminates the need to manually construct API payloads with nested components arrays."},
      {label:"Interactive messages — buttons and list messages",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to send WhatsApp messages that the recipient can respond to by tapping a button rather than typing, reducing friction and enabling structured responses. Two interactive types: Button messages — up to 3 quick-reply buttons; the recipient taps one and Plenix receives the button payload as a webhook event, which can trigger an automation (e.g. 'Confirm appointment' button triggers a calendar event creation). List messages — a header, body, footer, and a 'View options' button that opens a scrollable list of up to 10 sections with multiple rows each; useful for menus, service selections, or appointment time slots. Plenix's message composer includes a visual builder for both types — the user adds buttons or list rows without writing JSON. Incoming button responses are captured in the thread view with the selected option highlighted."},
      {label:"Webhook handling — inbound messages and status updates",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: Plenix needs to receive incoming WhatsApp messages and delivery status updates in real time so the user's conversation thread stays current. Plenix registers a webhook URL with Meta that receives POST notifications for: incoming messages (text, media, audio, interactive replies, and all other message types), message status updates (sent, delivered, read — displayed as single tick, double tick, blue tick on sent messages in the thread view), and template status updates (when a submitted template is approved or rejected by Meta). The webhook endpoint verifies the X-Hub-Signature-256 header on every request to ensure the payload is genuinely from Meta. Inbound media messages trigger a media download from the Meta media URL and store the file in Plenix's Files section linked to the contact's thread. All webhook events are processed in order and idempotently — duplicate deliveries from Meta do not create duplicate messages."},
      {label:"WhatsApp gateway via Ultramsg — simplified API layer",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The full WhatsApp Cloud API requires a Meta Business account, a verified business phone number, and direct API integration — which has significant setup friction. For users who want WhatsApp messaging without managing the Meta developer infrastructure directly, Plenix supports Ultramsg as an alternative gateway. Ultramsg is a WhatsApp API gateway that handles the underlying connection and exposes a simpler REST API. The user connects by entering their Ultramsg instance ID and token in Settings → WhatsApp. Plenix then uses Ultramsg's /sendMessage, /sendImage, /sendAudio, /sendDocument, and /sendVoice endpoints as a drop-in replacement for direct Meta API calls. The messaging experience in Plenix is identical regardless of which underlying method is used — the gateway choice is an implementation detail hidden from the end user. The Meta Cloud API path is recommended for production and scale; Ultramsg is recommended for faster setup and testing."},
      {label:"Audio and voice note messages",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user wants to send voice notes to WhatsApp contacts — the same way users send voice messages natively in WhatsApp — directly from Plenix. Audio messages are sent as OGG audio files encoded with the Opus codec (WhatsApp's native voice note format) or as MP3 for general audio. The Plenix message composer includes a voice recording button that captures audio via the browser microphone, encodes it to OGG/Opus, uploads it to the Meta media endpoint to obtain a media ID, then sends it as a WhatsApp audio message with type: voice set to true so it renders as a native voice note (with the waveform player) rather than a generic audio file attachment. The recipient's WhatsApp shows the message exactly as a normal voice note. Incoming audio messages received via webhook are downloaded and displayed in the Plenix thread with an inline audio player."},
      {label:"Contact and broadcast list management",status:"planned",desc:"Use case: The user needs to send WhatsApp messages to groups of contacts — supplier outreach, client updates, appointment reminders — without manually sending one at a time. Plenix's broadcast feature lets the user select contacts from the Contacts section (filtered by group, tag, or custom criteria), compose a message or select a pre-approved template, personalize variables per contact, and send to the entire list. Each message is sent as a separate individual conversation (not a group message) so replies come back privately to the user. Broadcast send status is tracked per recipient — showing delivered/read status for each contact in the batch. Opt-out handling: contacts who reply with a stop keyword are automatically flagged and excluded from future broadcasts. Send rate is throttled to respect Meta's per-phone-number message limits."}
    ]}
  ];
